n\" as marker — \"but\" is a linking word here (except). Plausible. \"always\" as modifier — fine. \"right there in the scene\": \"right\" and \"there\" as modifiers of the clause, \"in the scene\" relation. Hmm, \"right\" grading the linking word \"in\"? The settled case says a word that grades a linking word may be a modifier of the clause the relation belongs to. So \"right\" as modifier of $c1 is fine. \"there\" — place word, modifier fine.\n- Is anything lost? \"anywhere but on the right\" — the relation \"but on\" from clause to \"the right\" — the start could be the clause; fine. Acceptable. L8.\n\n**n=2**: \"delete linee e1\" — imperative \"delete\", complement $n1 = nominal \"linee\" dependent, head \"e1\". Hmm, \"linee e1\" — two bare nouns side by side (\"with id neck\", \"type radar\"): either may be the head. So head \"e1\" with dependent \"linee\" passes. L8.\n\n**n=3**: \"no, the cloud goes to the top — otherwise it hides the sun otherwise\"\n- @c1: declarative, discourse \"no\", modifier \"otherwise\", verb \"hides\", modifier \"otherwise\", subject \"it\", complement \"the sun\". @n1 \"the cloud\", @c2 declarative \"goes\" subject \"the cloud\", relation \"to\" from $c2 to \"the top\". Coordination of $c1 and $c2.\n- \"otherwise\" as modifier of the clause — fine (word after verb that is neither nominal nor marker is a modifier). \"no\" as discourse — settled: \"Thanks\" or \"Hey\" alone is a fragment with discourse; but \"no,\" at start... Hmm. \"no\" is a discourse word here? The rule says discourse holds words said to the listener: \"please\", \"hey\", \"thanks\". \"No\" at the start — like \"nope\" in n=51. Is \"no\" a content word? It's a negation... but there's no clause for it to negate at the start; it's said to the listener. Given n=51 is identical with \"nope\", and the settled cases mention \"Thanks\"/\"Hey\" alone as fragment with discourse — \"no\" as discourse of the following clause seems acceptable (like \"And\"/\"Then\"/\"So\" at start may be discourse). I'll accept.\n- The dash: \"the cloud goes to the top — otherwise it hides the sun otherwise\". The coordination joins $c1 (hides) and $c2 (goes). Order reversed but coordination has no order requirement. Both clauses tied. \"otherwise\" ×2 as modifiers. Acceptable. L8.\n\n**n=4**: \"a sad for me, eyebrow yellow default\"\n- @c1: fragment, discourse \"a\", complement $n1. $n1: dependents sad/eyebrow/yellow, head \"default\". relation \"for\" from $n1 to $n2 (\"me\").\n- \"a\" as discourse? \"a\" is an article — a content word? Hmm. \"a sad for me\" — \"a\" is the article of \"sad\". Holding \"a\" in discourse loses its place? L2 says \"A word with content is held in discourse\". Is \"a\" a word with content? It's an article. Hmm. In n=38, \"a looking out for me, eye auburn big\" — there \"a\" is held as modifier of the clause @c1 (nonfinite \"looking\"). In n=4, \"a\" is discourse. Compare n=63: \"a pointed for me, ear olive\" — @n1 head \"a\", complement $n1, relation \"for\" from $c1 to \"me\". There \"a\" is head of nominal.\n- For n=4: \"a sad for me, eyebrow yellow default\" — this looks like a garbled message (formalizer saw only words). The nominal is \"sad eyebrow yellow default\"? head \"default\" with dependents sad/eyebrow/yellow. And \"for me\" relation. \"a\" as discourse — the article \"a\" arguably has no content; it's a function word. L2 requires \"a word with content\". An article is not a word with content. I'd accept. Hmm, but is \"a\" said to the listener? Not exactly. But the rule for rejection: \"if you cannot quote one, the circuit is acceptable\" — I can quote 'a'. But is holding \"a\" in discourse a wrong statement? The rules say discourse holds words said to the listener. \"a\" is not said to the listener. Hmm, but L2 specifically: \"A word with content is held in discourse, so its place in the sentence is lost.\" \"a\" is not a word with content. The general principle: reject only wrong statement or lost explicit inf