wait — the message has \"do the error bar cap size smaller on the sales chart. title Q2 sales\" — \"title\" is the first word of a new sentence with no other verb, so it's the verb. Fine. $c5 is a separate clause — is it tied to anything? L6: \"A clause or a nominal is tied to nothing inside its sentence. Separate sentences need no tie.\" $c5 is a separate sentence (after a period), so no tie needed. OK.\n- \"as thick as it gets\": $c1 has modifiers \"as\" and \"thick\" — \"as\" held as modifier? The marker of r1 is also \"as\". Hmm, \"as thick as it gets\" — first \"as\" is part of \"as thick as\". The circuit holds \"as\" and \"thick\" as modifiers of $c1, and the relation marker \"as\" (the second \"as\") from $c1 to $c2. Wait, but each word must stand in exactly one value. r1 marker=\"as\" — which \"as\"? There are two \"as\" words. The program checked every word stands in exactly one value... presumably counts. If marker=\"as\" uses one \"as\" and modifier=\"as\" uses the other, fine. Plausible reading. Accept. L8.\n\n**n=7**: \"give me a seaside boardwalk in the sea wind at noon: three small white gulls right on the rail, a medium fisherman standing facing right next to the rail, right next to the fisherman, a medium brown dog, and right at the rail, two large black lamps\"\n- @c1 imperative \"give\", modifiers \"right\",\"right\", complements $n1 (me), $n2 (a seaside boardwalk). r1 \"in\" from $n2 to $n3 (the sea wind). r2 \"at\" from $c1 to $n4 (noon), attachment=ambiguous.\n- $n5 \"three small white gulls\", r3 \"on\" from $n5 to $n6 (the rail). \"right on the rail\" — \"right\" grading the linking word \"on\" — held as modifier of clause $c1. Per settled case: \"a word that grades a linking word may be a modifier of the clause the relation belongs to (the clause that holds the start of the relation...)\". Here r3 starts at $n5, not $c1. The rule says the modifier should be of the clause that holds the start of the relation — the relation r3 starts at nominal $n5 which is in... $n5 is tied via r7 to $n2, and $n2 is complement of $c1. Hmm. The rule about grading words: \"may be a modifier of the clause the relation belongs to (the clause that holds the start of the relation, not a participle clause that only stands near it)\". r3's start is $n5, a nominal. Which clause does the relation \"belong to\"? $n5 is tied to $k1 → r7 from $n2. So $n5 belongs to the clause $c1's complement $n2. The \"right\" is held as modifier of $c1. Plausibly fine — the clause $c1 is the one the gulls-rail relation belongs to (through $n2). The settled case's failure example was holding \"right\" as dependent of a nominal. Here it's a modifier of $c1. I'd accept.\n- $n7 \"a medium fisherman\", @c2 nonfinite \"standing\" modifier \"right\" complement $n10 (a medium brown dog)?? Wait — c2 has complement=$n10 where $n10 is \"a medium brown dog\". Hmm: \"a medium fisherman standing facing right next to the rail, right next to the fisherman, a medium brown dog\" — the list: gulls, fisherman, dog, lamps. The circuit: r8 from $n7 to $c2 (fisherman standing), r9 from $c2 to $c3 (facing), r4 \"next to\" from $c3 to $n8 (the rail), r5 \"next to\" from $c2 to $n9 (the fisherman). Hmm wait, that doesn't look right. Let me re-read.\n\n```\n@c2 syntax.clause form=nonfinite verb=\"standing\" modifier=\"right\" complement=$n10\n@c3 syntax.clause form=nonfinite verb=\"facing\" modifier=\"right\"\n@r4 syntax.relation marker=\"next to\" from=$c3 to=$n8   (the rail)\n@n8 the rail\n@r5 syntax.relation marker=\"next to\" from=$c2 to=$n9   (the fisherman)\n@n9 the fisherman\n@n10 a medium brown dog\n@r8 syntax.relation from=$n7 to=$c2   (fisherman → standing clause)\n@r9 syntax.relation from=$c2 to=$c3   (standing → facing)\n```\nWait, there are two @r8? Let me re-check: \"@r4 ... from=$c3 to=$n8\", \"@n8 the rail\", \"@r5 ... from=$c2 to=$n9\", \"@n9 the fisherman\", \"@n10 dog\", \"@k1 ... and ... $n5 $n7 $n12\", \"@r6 marker at from $n12 to $n11 (the rail)\", \"@n11 the rail\", \"@n12 two large black lamps\", \"@r7 from $n2 to $k1\", \"@r8 from $n7 to $c2\", \"@r9 from $c2 to $c3\".\n\nHmm, so \"facing right next to the rail\": c3 \"facing\" with modifier \"right\", relation \"next to\" from c3 to \"the rail\". OK. \"right next to the fisherman\": r5 \"next to\" from c2 (standing) to \"the fisherman\" — hmm, \"right next to the fisherman\" modifies... the fisherman himself? Actually in the list \"a medium fisherman standing facing right next to the rail, right next to the fisherman, a medium brown dog\" — hmm, \"right next to the fisherman\" is a separate phrase, maybe describing the dog's position. The circuit attaches it to c2 (standing). Not clearly wrong.\n\nBut: c2 \"standing\" has complement=$n10 (the dog). That's odd — \"a medium brown dog\" as complement of \"standing\"? The dog is a separate list item. Hmm, but \"standing\" taking a complement... Actually wait, maybe the formalizer read \"a medium fisherman standing ... a medium brown dog\" — no. Actually the complement of c2 being the dog is weird but is it a wrong statement? \"standing\" doesn't take \"a medium brown dog\" as complement in any reading. Hmm. But per rules, \"What a complement is to the verb... is not judged\". The question is whether a nominal is the complement of the wrong clause (L3). The dog tied as complement of \"standing\" — the words don't show the dog is a complement of \"standing\"; it's a list item. Hmm, but the dog is tied via r7? No — r7 is from $n2 to $k1. $k1 conjuncts: $n5 (gulls), $n7 (fisherman), $n12 (lamps). The dog $n10 is NOT in the coordination! It's only complement of c2. So the dog is inside the fisherman's standing clause. Is that a wrong statement? The message lists four items: gulls, fisherman, dog, lamps. The circuit has three in coordination and the dog as complement of \"standing\". Hmm. That's a plausible misreading? \"a medium fisherman standing ... a medium brown dog\" — could read as \"fisherman standing [with] a