oints\"). k2 \"and\" conjunct $c1 $c2. c2 imperative \"give\" complements $n6 (it), $n7 (a thin outline). r2 from $n1 to $n2 (new slide → the title), r3 from $n2 to $n3 (title → Q3 Review).\n- Hmm: \"new slide first, then the title Q3 Review, then Calibri at 36 points\" — the circuit ties $n1 to $n2 to $n3: \"new slide\" → \"the title\" → \"Q3 Review\". And $n4 (Calibri) in k1 with $n1. So the coordination \"then\" joins \"new slide\" and \"Calibri\" — but there are two \"then\"s: \"then the title Q3 Review, then Calibri\". The k1 has conjunction \"then\" (one) with conjuncts $n1 and $n4. The title $n2 is tied to $n1 via r2. Hmm — the structure is a bit off: \"then the title Q3 Review\" is the second item, \"then Calibri at 36 points\" the third. The circuit puts \"the title\" as related to \"new slide\" and \"Calibri\" as second conjunct. The other \"then\" is held as modifier of $c1. Is a conjunct wrong? The coordination k1 joins $n1 and $n4 with \"then\" — the wire that ends right before the conjunction... The settled case: \"The earlier wire a coordination joins is the wire that ends right before the conjunction, or a wire that holds it.\" Before the second \"then\" is \"Q3 Review\" ($n3); before the first \"then\" is \"first\" (modifier of c1). Hmm. The coordination joins $n1 (new slide) and $n4 (Calibri). Grammar: \"new slide first, then X, then Y, and Z\" — items are: new slide, the title Q3 Review, Calibri at 36 points, give it a thin outline. The circuit's k1 = {new slide, Calibri} missing \"the title Q3 Review\" as a conjunct (it's attached via r2/r3 to $n1). Is that a wrong conjunct? \"A coordination has a wrong conjunct\" — the conjuncts present are $n1 and $n4; both are items. The title is not a conjunct but tied to $n1. Hmm — this is like \"profit by ship mode and customer segment\" where several earlier wires allowed. I think acceptable. L8.\n\n**n=10**: \"go for error bar caps a size larger on the sales chart and then have the line thicker\"\n- @c1 imperative \"go\". r1 \"for\" from $c1 to $n1. $n1: dependents error/bar/head caps + dependents a/size/larger — \"a size larger\" held as dependents of \"caps\"! Settled case: \"Words that grade a graded word ('a bit longer', 'way shorter', 'a little smaller') are modifier words of the clause... A nominal whose head is the graded word ('a bit longer' with head 'longer') or the lone grading word ('way') fails: L3.\" Also: \"A word after a nominal that is a complement of its clause and says how the thing should be is a modifier of the clause... So is the word after a measure: 'the dog a size bigger' with 'two spaces' and 'a size' nominals of their own. Held as a dependent of the nominal, the word becomes part of the description by which the thing is found: L3.\"\n- Here \"a size larger\" after \"error bar caps\" (the complement of \"go for\"): held as dependents of the nominal $n1. Per the settled case \"the dog a size bigger\" — \"a size\" should be a nominal of its own, and \"bigger\" a modifier of the clause; held as dependent of the nominal → L3. Here \"a\", \"size\", \"larger\" are all dependents of \"caps\". That matches the failure: L3. Note must quote words: 'a size larger' held as dependents of 'error bar caps'... The note should quote words of the message in single quotes. E.g., \"'a size larger' is held as dependents of the nominal 'caps', but the words say the caps should be larger\". Grade L3.\n- Also check the rest: r2 \"on\" from $n1 to \"the sales chart\" — fine. k1 \"and\" c1 c2. c2 imperative, modifier \"then\", verb \"have\", modifier \"thicker\", complement \"the line\". \"have the line thicker\" — \"thicker\" as modifier of clause, fine (like \"make the box red\": red is modifier). OK. So the only problem is \"a size larger\" as dependents. L3.\n\n**n=11**: \"how about 2 kites in a row up in the sky all facing right? — that's how this drawing goes\"\n- @n1: dependents how/about/2, head \"kites\". r1 \"in\" from $n1 to \"a row\". r2 \"up in\" from $