nate clause tied to nothing is L6. Any relation to its clause or to the nominal it describes is enough.\" Here \"facing right\" describes the kites — but there's no relation from $n1 to c1. So c1 is untied → L6? But wait, c1 has subject $n1 — does the subject count as tying? The subject is inside the clause. Hmm, \"A clause or a nominal is tied to nothing inside its sentence\" — c1's sentence... the clause itself contains $n1 as subject. I think the subject relation is internal. The settled case about participles: \"a group called g2\", \"a bird facing left\" — the participle clause must be tied to the thing. Here \"all facing right\" — the circuit gave it subject $n1 (\"2 kites\"), which arguably ties it to the kites — the subject IS the tie? Hmm. In n=7, \"facing right next to the rail\" was c3 tied via r9 from c2 and r4 to rail. In n=26, c2 \"facing\" tied via r6 from $n4.\n- In n=11, \"all facing right\" — c1 has subject=$n1. If subject counts as the connection, then it's tied. Actually, hmm: a clause whose subject is a nominal — the nominal is a wire of the clause (subject key), so they're connected within the circuit. I'd say that counts as tied (the clause is about the kites). Also \"how about\" as dependents of the nominal \"kites\" — \"how about 2 kites\" as one nominal, head \"kites\", dependents \"how\",\"about\",\"2\". Settled: question word stands where grammar puts it — \"what colour\" dependent in nominal. \"how about\" — plausible as dependents. Hmm, but the question form: \"how about ... ?\" is a question — should there be an interrogative clause? The settled case: \"A clause that asks, at the start of a sentence ('where is it?'), is interrogative.\" Here \"How about 2 kites...?\" — the whole message is a question but the circuit wrote no interrogative clause; the second sentence \"that's how this drawing goes\" is declarative. Is the first part a clause at all? \"How about 2 kites in a row up in the sky all facing right?\" — could be a fragment (nominal \"2 kites...\" with modifiers) — like \"how about thinner grid lines\" in n=47 where c1 is fragment with modifier \"how\" and relation \"about\" to $n1. In n=47, the circuit made \"how about thinner grid lines\" a fragment c1 with r1 \"about\" from c1 to $n1. In n=11, instead, \"how about 2 kites\" is inside the nominal. Both plausible? In n=47 \"how about\" precedes a nominal; the fragment clause holds \"how\". In n=11, \"how about 2\" are dependents of \"kites\". Hmm. \"2\" as dependent of \"kites\" — a number right after... \"how about 2 kites\" — 2 is before the noun, a quantity — \"a quantity phrase before a noun may be held as dependent words of that noun\" (\"a pair of trees\", numbers among them). So \"2\" dependent of \"kites\" passes. \"how about\" as dependents — plausible.\n- So the question: is the lack of an interrogative clause a problem? The message is a question mark... \"Whether a question is meant as a request\" is not judged. The form of the fragment: the message part \"how about 2 kites...\" has no verb except \"facing\" (nonfinite). So the main part is a fragment — fine, form=fragment would be needed but the circuit has no clause for it; the nominal $n1 stands alone with relations. Is a nominal tied to nothing? $n1 has r1, r2 outgoing, and c1's subject. Its sentence: the first sentence. $n1 is tied (r1, r2). OK.\n- But c1: nonfinite \"facing\" with subject $n1, modifier all/right — untied to any relation. Hmm. But its subject is $n1... I think within the schema, the subject wire connects the clause to the nominal — the circuit graph: c1 → subject → $n1. So c1 is tied to $n1. I'd say that counts. Also compare n=64: c2 nonfinite \"fading\" subject=$n4, r4 from $c1 to $c2. There the relation exists too.\n- Let me compare with n=150: c1 nonfinite \"inverted\", r3 from $n2 to $c1 — relation exists.\n- In n=11, no relation to c1, only subject. Does subject count as a tie? L6: \"A clause or a nominal is tied to nothing inside its sentence.\" The