 block 1), r9 from $n4 to $n5.\n- Hmm, this is convoluted. \"est block 1 down to the bottom, next est block 1 right until just short of block 12\" — \"est\" is probably a misspelling of \"test\"? or \"west\"? Actually \"est block\" — in n=22 \"please est the Shell block\" — \"est\" as a verb? In n=12 the circuit treats \"est block\" as a nominal (dependent est, head block) — like \"block 1\" with \"est\" as a dependent. Hmm, or \"est\" could be a verb (\"est block 1 down...\" = \"set block 1 down...\"). Misspelt word held where it stands when words don't settle — \"A misspelt or unknown word, held where it stands, when the words do not settle what it is\" passes. \"est\" as dependent of \"block\" — plausible.\n- Structure: c1 fragment with complement $n1 (\"est block 1\"), modifier down; r1 \"to\" to the bottom. Then \"next est block 1 right until...\" — n4 \"next est block 1\" tied from $n1 (r8), r2 \"until\" from n4 to \"just short of block 12\". c2 \"Then est block 1 up ... left until...\" complement $k1 (coordination of n9 \"est block 1\" and n14 \"est block 1\"), r4 until c3, r5 until c4.\n- Wait, c2 has complement=$k1, and modifiers up/left — \"Then est block 1 up until it almost touches block 7, then est block 1 left until it is clear of block 4\". So c2 covers both \"up\" and \"left\" moves, with the two \"est block 1\" nominals as conjuncts. r4 \"until\" from c2 to c3 (touches block 7) — but the \"until it almost touches block 7\" belongs to the \"up\" move; \"until it is clear of block 4\" to the \"left\" move (r5 from $n14). Hmm, r4 from $c2 — the clause — acceptable (relation can start at the clause).\n- Is anything wrong? The coordination k1 joins n9 and n14 with \"then\" — \"then est block 1 left\" — the conjunction \"then\" between them. OK. c2 complement $k1 — a coordination as complement. Plausible.\n- \"just short of block 12\": n6 \"just short\" head short, dependent just; r3 \"of\" from n6 to block 12. Fine.\n- Overall: messy but each piece tied. Any wrong statement? \"down to the bottom\" — r1 from c1 to bottom, fine. I'll accept L8. Hmm, but wait — c1 and c2: c1 is a fragment, c2 is a fragment with discourse \"Then\". Is c2 tied to anything? c2 has r4 to c3; c2's complement k1 contains n9, n14 which are tied (r11 n9→n10, r13 n14→n15). But is c2 itself tied to c1 or anything? L6: a clause tied to nothing inside its sentence. The second sentence starts with \"Then est block 1 up...\" — it's a new sentence (after period). \"Separate sentences need no tie.\" c2 is the second sentence's fragment. OK. And c1 — first sentence, tied via r1. OK. L8.\n\n**n=13**: \"from now on, ahve the boy in the picture stay a bit above the horizon line\"\n- r1 \"from\" from $c1 to $n1 (\"now\"). c1 declarative, modifier \"on\", discourse \"ahve\", verb \"stay\", subject $n2 (\"the boy\"), complement $n4 (\"a bit\"). r2 \"in\" from $n2 to \"the picture\". r3 \"above\" from $n4 to \"the horizon line\".\n- \"ahve\" (misspelling of \"have\") held as discourse — it's a content word (verb \"have\")! \"ahve the boy ... stay\" = \"have the boy stay\". The circuit wrote c1 with verb \"stay\", subject \"the boy\", and \"ahve\" as discourse. Per L2: \"A word with content is held in discourse, so its place in the sentence is lost.\" \"ahve\" is a verb with content — the causative \"have\". Holding it in discourse loses that the boy is made to stay. Hmm — but the settled case for \"have\": \"Have the legend switched on.\" — \"have\" is the verb of an imperative clause. Here \"ahve the boy in the picture stay a bit above the horizon line\" — \"have\" is the main verb, \"stay\" the nonfinite complement. The circuit made \"stay\" the main declarative verb with subject \"the boy\" and \"ahve\" discourse. That's like the \"let's\" case: holding the causative as discourse loses it. The settled case for let's says holding \"let's\" as discourse of an imperative clause of \"add\" also passes. Hmm! \"let's add a box\": \"let\" is the verb; ... Holding \"let's\" or \"let us\" as discourse words of an imperative clause of \"add\" also passes. So by analogy, \"have the boy stay\" with \"have\" as discourse of a clause of \"stay\" might pass?? But there the clause of \"add\" is imperative (a command); here c1 is declarative with subject \"the boy\" — \"the boy stay a bit above the horizon line\" as a declarative statement is wrong: the message says \"have the boy stay\" (causative), not \"the boy stays\". Hmm, but the settled case says the discourse option passes for let's. For \"have\", the settled case is \"Have the legend switched on.\" where \"have\" must be the verb of imperative. Here the message is \"ahve the boy in the picture stay a bit above...\" — no question mark; it's a command: \"have the boy stay...\". So \"ahve\" should be the verb of an imperative clause with \"the boy\" complement and \"stay\" nonfinite. The circuit instead: declarative \"stay\" with subject \"the boy\" and \"ahve\" discourse. Per the let's analogy, holding \"let's\" as discourse of imperative \"add\" passes — but there the finite clause is imperative (command). Here the finite clause is declarative with the boy as subject — that states the boy stays, which the message does not say (L4-ish: a subject the words do not show? The words do show \"the boy\" as... hmm, in \"have the boy stay\", \"the boy\" is the subject of \"stay\" in the accusative-with-infinitive construction — actually \"the boy\" IS the subject of \"stay\" grammatically!). So \"the boy stay\" with subject \"the boy\" is grammatically the embedded clause; the main verb \"have\" is lost to discourse. The information lost: that this is a command to make the boy stay. Is that \"lost explicit information\"? The causative \"have\" is a content word held in discourse → L2 explicitly: \"A word with content is held in discourse, so its place in the sentence is lost.\" \"ahve\" is a word with content (a verb). I'll grade L2. Note: \"'ahve' is held as a discourse word, but it is the verb of the command, so its place in the sentence is lost\". \n- Hmm wait, but compare the let's case again: \"Holding 'let's' or 'let us' as discourse words of an imperative clause of 'add' also passes.\" The difference: there, \"add\" remains a command (imperative), so the message's command force is preserved; \"let's\" as discourse is acceptable. Here, \"stay\" is written as declarative — the command force is lost and \"ahve\" (the actual command verb) is in discourse. The rule L1 also says: \"'s' or 'us' as the subject of 'let', or 'add' written as a declarative clause, fails: L1.\" By analogy, \"stay\" written as a declarative clause with the object as subject — hmm, that's the L1 failure for let's (\"add written as a declarative clause fails\"). Here \"stay\" is written as declarative with \"the boy\" as subject — analogous to \"add written as declarative\" → L1? In the let's case, \"'s\"/\"us\" as subject of \"let\" fails L1; \"add\" as declarative fails L1. Here: \"ahve\" in discourse + \"stay\" declarative with subject \"the boy\". The parallel failure: the main verb \"ahve\" is not the verb of any clause (L5?) — \"A clause of the message is not written: its verb is held as a modifier, a dependent, a head or a discourse word, and no clause has it as verb.\" \"ahve\" is held as discourse word and no clause has it as verb → L5! Hmm, L5 fits exactly: the verb \"ahve\" is held as a discourse word. But wait — is \"ahve\" a clause of the message? \"ahve the boy stay...\" — yes, \"have\" is the main verb of the command. L5: \"its verb is held as a modifier, a dependent, a head or a discourse word, and no clause has it as verb\" — exactly. But L2 also fits (\"a word with content is held in discourse\"). Grades: \"Give L8, or the first of L1 to L6 that applies.\" L1 first... L1 doesn't apply (form of c1 — declarative vs imperative — is that wrong? The clause written is \"the boy stay...\" which is a command in the message. \"a clear question written as imperative or declarative, or the reverse\" — the message's command written as declarative — that's L1-ish: \"Its form is wrong: a clear question written as imperative or declarative, or the reverse\". A command written as declarative... \"or the reverse\" covers declarative written as imperative? Hmm, \"a clear question written as imperative or declarative, or the reverse\" — question vs statement. A command is imperative; written as declarative — the reverse of \"declarative written as imperative\"? The examples: \"a clear question written as imperative or declarative, or the reverse\" — I read it as: form wrong in either direction between question/statement/command. \"Have the legend switched on.\" written as interrogative fails L1 — that's a command written as question → L1. So a command written as declarative → L1 too. Here c1 is declarative but the message (no question mark) is a command \"ahve the boy ... stay\". So L1 applies? The clause's form is wrong: command written as declarative. Hmm, but which clause — the circuit wrote c1 as the clause of \"stay\"; the message's command is \"ahve...\". The settled let's case says \"add written as a declarative clause fails: L1\". By analogy, \"stay\" written as declarative (when the message is a command with \"have\") fails L1. Hmm, but in the let's case, the acceptable alternative was imperative \"add\" with let's as discourse; the failure was declarative \"add\". Here \"stay\" is declarative → L1? Or is the subject wrong: \"the boy\" as subject of a declarative — in the message \"the boy\" is not the subject of the main clause (there is no main clause with subject; it's \"have [boy] [stay]\"). L4: \"a subject the words do not show\". Hmm.\n- The first applicable grade in order L1..L6: L1 if we consider the form wrong. Let me think again: is \"the boy stay a bit above the horizon line\" clearly a command? \"ahve the boy in the picture stay a bit above the horizon line\" — with \"ahve\" misspelt, but clearly \"have ... stay\" = imperative causative. The circuit's c1: declarative, subject \"the boy\", verb \"stay\", complement \"a bit\". If we hold \"ahve\" as discourse (like \"let's\"), then the clause of \"stay\" should be imperative (\"stay\") per the let's analogy — but here it's declarative with a subject. The let's rule: \"'s' or 'us' as the subject of 'let', or 'add' written as a declarative clause, fails: L1.\" So writing the nonfinite verb's clause as declarative with the object as subject fails L1. Here c1 \"stay\" declarative subject \"the boy\" — analogous → L1. Hmm, but actually in \"let's add a box\", \"add\" is nonfinite (to add). The acceptable: \"let\" verb, \"'s\" complement, \"add\" nonfinite tied. Or let's as discourse + \"add\" imperative. Failure: \"add\" declarative. Here \"stay\" after \"ahve the boy\" is nonfinite (bare infinitive). Written as declarative with subject — that's the failure pattern. I'll go L1? Or L2/L5?\n- Order: L1 first. Does L1 apply? L1: \"A clause that the circuit writes is wrong. Its form is wrong: ... a clear question written as imperative or declarative, or the reverse\". The clause c1 as written: \"the boy stay a bit above the horizon line\" declarative — the message doesn't say the boy stays; it says have the boy stay. Is the form wrong? The message's clause is imperative (command). Written declarative — \"or the reverse\" — I'd say yes, L1. Also \"'ahve'\" as discourse — L2. And no clause has \"ahve\" as verb — L5. First applicable: L1. Hmm, but is it really? Let me reconsider — maybe the intended reading is simpler: \"ahve\" is a misspelt word, \"held where it stands, when the words do not settle what it is\" — but here the words do settle: \"ahve the boy stay\" is clearly \"have the boy stay\". The formalizer put \"ahve\" in discourse — that loses the command. I think L2 is the most defensible: \"ahve\" is a word with content held in discourse. But L1 comes first if the form is wrong. The clause c1's form: declarative. The message: a command. Per the \"Have the legend switched on\" settled case: written as interrogative it fails L1. So command written as declarative fails L1. But here the clause written is \"stay\", not \"ahve\"... The circuit writes one clause for the whole message. Its form is declarative; the message is a command → form wrong → L1. I'll go with L1. Hmm, wait — but also consider: maybe \"ahve\" could be treated as unknown/misspelt held in discourse? No — discourse is for words said to the listener; \"ahve\" is clearly the verb. The safest: L1 with note quoting 'ahve' — \"'ahve' is the command verb but is held as a discourse word and its clause 'stay' is written declarative with 'the boy' as subject\". Actually for the note, I need to quote words in single quotes and name the wrong statement. Note: \"'ahve the boy ... stay' is a command, but 'ahve' is held in discourse and 'stay' is written as a declarative clause with 'the boy' as subject\". That quotes message words. Grade L1.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider once more. The let's settled case explicitly allows \"let's\" as discourse of an imperative clause of \"add\". If the formalizer had written c1 as imperative \"stay\" with \"ahve\" discourse, it would pass. It wrote declarative → per the settled case, \"add written as a declarative clause fails: L1\". So L1. Yes, L1.\n\n**n=14**: \"I'd like a black bird up above the house there\"\n- c1 declarative, auxiliary \"'d\", verb \"like\", modifier \"up\", subject \"I\", complement $n2 (\"a black bird\"). r1 \"above\" from $n2 to $n3 (\"the house\" dependent \"there\"). \n- \"there\" as dependent of \"the house\" — settled: \"A word of place after a nominal ('near the lake there', 'the one above') may be a dependent of that nominal or a modifier of the clause: grammar lets it describe either.\" So \"there\" dependent of \"house\" passes. \"up\" modifier of clause — fine. \"above\" relation from bird to house — fine. L8.\n\n**n=15**: \"I also need you to set Reject left of Archive so that someone reading from left to right reaches Reject last\"\n- c1 declarative \"need\", modifier also, subject I. r1 \"to\" from c1 to c2. c2 nonfinite \"set\", modifier \"left\", subject \"you\", complement $n3 (\"Reject\"). r2 \"of\" from $c2 to $n4 (\"Archive\"). Hmm — \"left of Archive\": settled case: \"'left of the hill', 'right of the road': 'left of' may be one marker, or 'left' a nominal or a clause modifier with the relation marked 'of' starting at the clause or at 'left'.\" Here: \"left\" is a modifier of c2, and r2 \"of\" from $c2 (the clause) to Archive. That matches the second option: relation marked \"of\" starting at the clause. Passes.\n- r3 \"so that\" from c2 to c4. c3 nonfinite \"reading\", r4 \"from\" from c3 to \"left\", r5 \"to\" (attachment ambiguous) from c3 to \"right\". r6 from $n5 (someone) to c3. c4 declarative \"reaches\", modifier \"last\", subject \"someone\", complement \"Reject\".\n- Wait — c4's subject is $n5 \"someone\", and r6 ties $n5 to c3. Fine. \"reaches Reject last\" — \"last\" modifier. OK. c3 tied to c4? c3 is inside \"so that\" clause c4? r3 \"so that\" from c2 to c4 — c4 is the \"so that\" clause. c3 (reading) — tied via r6 from someone; someone is subject of c4. Is c3 tied to c4? Hmm, \"so that someone reading from left to right reaches Reject last\" — \"someone reading...\" is the subject of \"reaches\". The circuit: c4 subject $n5, r6 $n5→c3. So c3 hangs off $n5 which is in c4. Tied enough. L8.\n\n**n=16**: \"the dog on the path in front of the hill: face left\"\n- n1 \"the dog\", r1 \"on\" n1→n2 (the path), r2 \"in\" n2→n3 (front), r3 \"of\" n3→n4 (the hill). n5 nominal head \"face\" dependent \"left\". c1 fragment complement $n1. r4 from $n1 to $n5.\n- \"face left\" — \"face\" as head of nominal with dependent \"left\", tied to the dog. Hmm — \"face left\" is a command (\"face left\"). Settled: \"A command needs its verb. When the first word of a message with no other verb can be a noun or a verb ('book design review'), it is the verb; held as a noun in a fragment, the command is lost: L1.\" Here the message: \"the dog on the path in front of the hill: face left\" — after the colon, \"face left\" — \"face\" can be a noun or verb. Is there another verb in the message? No. So \"face\" is the first word of a part with no other verb that can be a noun or verb → it is the verb; held as a noun (head of nominal $n5), the command is lost → L1. Hmm — \"the first word of a message with no other verb\" — \"face\" is not the first word of the message, but of the part after the colon. The rule says \"of a message\". Hmm. \"face left\" after a colon is clearly a command (imperative). The circuit holds \"face\" as head of a nominal tied to the dog — like \"the dog facing left\"? No, it's \"face left\" = imperative. Per the settled case's spirit (\"A command needs its verb\"), \"face\" should be the verb of an imperative clause. Held as a noun in a fragment → L1. But strictly the settled case says \"first word of a message\". Here the colon part... I think the intent: \"face left\" is a command; the circuit made it a nominal. That loses the command. L1? Or is \"face left\" a fragment describing the dog (\"the dog, face left\" = facing left)? With the colon, it's an instruction. Hmm. The formalizer saw only words — punctuation stands in no wire, but the colon... The words \"face left\" after a noun phrase — could be read as a reduced relative \"which faces left\" or an imperative. Grammar allows both? \"make A blue and B green\" — the second is elliptical imperative. Here \"the dog ... : face left\" — I'd read it as imperative \"face left\". The circuit's reading as a nominal tied to the dog (like an appositive \"face left\") is... plausible? \"the dog on the path in front of the hill: face left\" — as a label? Hmm.\n- The settled case: \"A command needs its verb. When the first word of a message with no other verb can be a noun or a verb ('book design review', 'schedule the kickoff'), it is the verb; held as a noun in a fragment, the command is lost: L1.\" The phrase \"of a message\" — but here we have \"text\" = whole message. \"face\" is not the first word. But the second part after the colon is a separate sentence. Hmm. Compare n=60: \"mouth — add a box, please\" — c2 fragment complement \"mouth\", c1 imperative \"add\". There \"add\" is the verb. In n=16, if \"face\" were meant as imperative, the circuit should have a clause with verb \"face\". It doesn't — \"face\" is a head of a nominal → the command is lost → per the settled case pattern, L1. But the settled case is about the first word of a message... \"face\" is the first word of the segment after the colon. I think the spirit applies: a command needs its verb. Alternatively, the reading \"the dog ... face left\" as a fragment where \"face left\" is a nominal describing the dog's face... \"face left\" = \"face [to the] left\"? Eh.\n- Hmm, let me compare with n=40: \"missing: one bench on the left\" — \"missing\" as dependent of \"bench\" — fragment. That's a label-style message. And n=16 similar style: \"the dog ...: face left\" — hmm, \"face left\" could be a property: face left (the face is left?)... In these diagram commands, \"face left\" means \"make it face left\". I'll grade L1: \"'face' is the command verb of 'face left', but it is held as the head of a nominal tied to 'the dog', so the command is lost\". Wait, but the settled case says \"When the first word of a message with no other verb can be a noun or a verb\" — \"face left\" is a part with no other verb, and \"face\" can be noun or verb → it is the verb. The message contains that part. I'm fairly confident: L1.\n\nHmm, actually wait. Let me reconsider. \"the dog on the path in front of the hill: face left\" — maybe it means \"the dog ...: [its] face [to the] left\"? That's strained. The imperative reading is natural. And the settled case explicitly covers noun-or-verb first words of verb-less parts. L1.\n\n**n=17**: \"Describe how order value moved over the quarter\"\n- c1 imperative \"Describe\". c2 declarative, modifier \"how\", verb \"moved\", subject $n1 (\"order value\"). r1 \"over\" from c2 to \"the quarter\". r2 from c1 to c2.\n- Settled: \"A clause that opens with a question word after a verb of telling or asking ('tell me which events are scheduled') must be tied to that verb's clause; any relation is enough. Untied it is L6.\" Here r2 ties c2 to c1. Also \"A clause that opens with a question word inside another clause ... states: its form is declarative\" — c2 is declarative. Good. L8.\n\n**n=18**: \"sidee the green block one space left and a quarter space up but never touching the blue block\"\n- n1 \"sidee\" (head). n2 \"the green block\". n3 nominal \"one space\" + dependent \"left\" — head \"space\", dependents \"one\", \"left\". k1 \"and\" conjunct n3, n4 (\"a quarter space\"). c1 nonfinite \"touching\", modifier \"up\", negation \"never\", complement \"the blue block\". k2 \"but\" conjunct c2, c1. n5 blue block. c2 fragment complement $n1. r1 n1→n2. r2 n2→k1.\n- \"sidee\" is probably \"slide\" misspelt — \"slide the green block one space left and a quarter space up but never touching the blue block\". The circuit holds \"sidee\" as a head of nominal $n1, with c2 fragment complement $n1, and r1 from $n1 to $n2 (the green block). So \"sidee the green block\" → nominal \"sidee\" tied to \"the green block\". The command verb \"sidee\" is held as a head of a nominal — L5? \"A clause of the message is not written: its verb is held as a modifier, a dependent, a head or a discourse word, and no clause has it as verb.\" \"sidee\" (slide) is the verb of the command, held as head of a nominal, no clause has it as verb → L5. But wait — \"A misspelt or unknown word, held where it stands, when the words do not settle what it is\" — the words do settle: \"sidee the green block ... \" — hmm, does \"sidee\" settle as a verb? \"sidee the green block one space left\" — with \"sidee\" as noun, \"sidee the green block\" would be two nominals... The message pattern matches \"move block 7 three spaces left\" where the first word is the verb. \"sidee\" is a misspelling of \"slide\". A command needs its verb. Per the settled case: \"When the first word of a message with no other verb can be a noun or a verb ('book design review', 'schedule the kickoff'), it is the verb; held as a noun in a fragment, the command is lost: L1.\" Is \"sidee\" the first word of the message? Yes! \"sidee the green block...\" — first word. Can it be a noun or a verb? It's misspelt; unknown word — \"held where it stands, when the words do not settle what it is\". Hmm — the words: \"sidee the green block one space left and ...\" — the pattern \"VERB the X ...\" — \"sidee\" could be a verb (like \"slide\"). With no other verb in the message (touching is nonfinite), the first word that can be a verb is the verb → held as noun → command lost → L1. But is \"sidee\" recognizable as a verb? It's misspelt \"slide\". The formalizer saw only words. \"sidee\" as unknown word — but the construction \"sidee the green block\" with \"the\" after — \"sidee\" + nominal → verb reading is natural. I think L1: the command's verb 'sidee' is held as the head of a nominal. Hmm, or L5 (verb held as head). The settled case says L1 for this pattern (\"held as a noun in a fragment, the command is lost: L1\"). Here c2 is a fragment with complement $n1 (sidee nominal). So L1.\n- Wait, but actually — could \"sidee\" be a noun meaning something? Like \"side\" + e? \"sidee the green block\" — no. Given the message is clearly a slide command, L1. Note: \"'sidee' is the verb of the command but is held as the head of a nominal, so no clause has it as verb and the command is lost\". Grade L1 (per settled case). Hmm, L5 vs L1: the settled case explicitly assigns L1 for \"held as a noun in a fragment, the command is lost\". Go L1.\n\n**n=19**: \"half the stand-up was grumbling that the numbers were impossible to read from the corridor screen; to settle it, go for marker spacing wider on the order scatter — while you're at it, have the error bars magenta going forward and then show both grid lines\"\n- c1 declarative, modifier \"half\", auxiliary \"was\", verb \"grumbling\", subject \"the stand-up\". r1 \"that\" from c1 to c2. c2 declarative \"were\", modifier \"impossible\", subject \"the numbers\". r2 \"to\" from c2 to c3 (nonfinite \"read\"). r3 \"from\" from c3 to \"the corridor screen\". r4 \"to\" from c5 to c4 (nonfinite \"settle\", complement \"it\"). c5 imperative \"go\". r5 \"for\" from c5 to n5 (\"marker spacing\" + dependent \"wider\"). Hmm — \"marker spacing wider\" — \"wider\" as dependent of the nominal \"spacing\"! Settled case: \"A word after a nominal that is a complement of its clause and says how the thing should be is a modifier of the clause... Held as a dependent of the nominal, the word becomes part of the description by which the thing is found: L3.\" Here \"marker spacing\" is the object of \"go for\" (complement of c5 via r5 relation end), and \"wider\" says how the spacing should be → should be modifier of the clause; held as dependent of \"spacing\" → L3. Same as n=10. \n- Also check: \"wider\" — in n=10 it was \"a size larger\" as dependents. Here just \"wider\" dependent of \"spacing\". Per the settled case (\"font bigger\", \"bar gap small\" — word after complement nominal → modifier of clause; held as dependent → L3). Yes L3. Note: \"'wider' is held as a dependent of 'spacing', but the words say the marker spacing should be wider, so it belongs as a modifier of the clause\".\n- Continue checking rest: r6 \"on\" from n5 to \"the order scatter\". r7 \"while\" from c7 to c6. c6 \"'re\" subject you, r8 \"at\" c6→it. c7 imperative \"have\", modifier \"magenta\", complement \"the error bars\". r9 from c7 to c8 (nonfinite \"going\" modifier forward). k1 \"and\" conjunct c1 c5 c7 c9. c9 imperative modifier \"then\" verb \"show\" complement \"both grid lines\".\n- Hmm — k1 joins c1 (grumbling), c5 (go), c7 (have), c9 (show) with \"and\". But the message: \"half the stand-up was grumbling...; to settle it, go for... — while you're at it, have ... and then show both grid lines\". The \"and\" is between \"have the error bars magenta going forward\" and \"then show both grid lines\" — so \"and\" should join c7 and c9, not all four. The settled case: \"The earlier wire a coordination joins is the wire that ends right before the conjunction, or a wire that holds it. A coordination with a clause between two of its conjuncts that belongs to none of them fails: L3.\" Here k1's conjuncts: c1, c5, c7, c9. Between c5 and c7 is c6 (the \"while you're at it\" clause) — c6 belongs to neither? Hmm, the conjunction \"and\" (in \"going forward and then show\") — the wire ending right before it is c8/r9 (going forward)... The coordination includes c1 and c5 as conjuncts with \"and\" — but the message's \"and\" only joins the \"have...\" and \"show...\" parts. Per the settled case (the \"I walked the team through Sales …; … so as a first pass make the matrix …, then we revisit next week, and give it the title …\" example): \"'and' cannot join 'give' with 'I walked' across 'make'\" — L3. Here similarly, \"and\" cannot join c1/c5 with c7/c9 across c6/c8. So the coordination k1 with conjuncts c1, c5, c7, c9 is wrong → L3.\n- Hmm, but wait — is that over-strict? The settled case says a coordination with a clause between two conjuncts that belongs to none of them fails. Between c5 and c7 sits c6 (\"you're at it\") — c6 is tied via r7 \"while\" from c7. So c6 belongs to c7's relation... \"belongs to none of them\" — c6 is inside the \"while\" relation starting at c7. Hmm, the example in the rules: \"and give it the title\" — \"and\" cannot join \"give\" with \"I walked\" across \"make\". In our case, the \"and\" is at \"going forward and then show\" — joining \"have...\" and \"show...\". The circuit's k1 also includes c1 and c5 as conjuncts — those are earlier sentences. The conjunction word \"and\" in the message stands between \"forward\" and \"then\" — the wire right before it is c8 (going) or c7 (have). So the coordination should join c7 (and c8) with c9. Including c1 and c5 as conjuncts of this \"and\" is wrong — c1 and c5 are separated by the semicolon and the dash; they're not joined by \"and\". Per the settled case, L3.\n- But hold on — which grade comes first? We already have L3 for \"wider\" as dependent. So L3 anyway. Note can mention both or one. The note must quote words and name the wrong statement. I'll focus on the clearer one. Both are L3. Note: \"'wider' is held as a dependent of 'spacing' though the words say the spacing should become wider; the coordination 'and' also joins 'go' and 'grumbling' across 'while you're at it'\". Hmm, one sentence. Maybe: \"'wider' is held as a dependent of the nominal 'marker spacing' instead of a modifier of the clause, and 'and' joins 'go' with 'have' across the 'while you're at it' clause\". That's one sentence quoting message words. OK.\n- Actually, let me double check \"wider\" — is \"marker spacing wider\" like \"font bigger\"? \"go for marker spacing wider on the order scatter\" — yes, \"go for X wider\" = make X wider. \"wider\" after the complement nominal \"marker spacing\" → modifier of clause. Held as dependent → L3 per settled case. Confirmed.\n\n**n=20**: \"the top right corner needs to stay clear, just the way the drawing looks\"\n- n1 \"the top right corner\". c1 declarative \"needs\" subject n1. r1 \"to\" from c1 to c2 (nonfinite \"stay\", modifier \"clear\", complement n2 \"just the way\"). Hmm — c2 complement=$n2 where n2 = \"just the way\" (dependents just/the, head way). Then r2 from n2 to c3 (\"the drawing looks\"). \n- \"just the way the drawing looks\" — the complement of \"stay\" should be \"clear\" with \"just the way...\" as a relation. The circuit: c2 modifier \"clear\", complement $n2 (\"just the way\"). Hmm — \"stay clear just the way the drawing looks\" — \"just the way the drawing looks\" is a modifier/comparison clause, not a complement nominal of \"stay\". The circuit made \"just the way\" a nominal (head \"way\") with dependents \"just\",\"the\" — merging \"just the way\" into a nominal and tying it via r2 to c3 (looks). Hmm. Is that wrong? \"the way the drawing looks\" is a nominal-ish (\"the way...\"), and \"just\" grades it. The relation r2 from n2 to c3 with no marker — \"the way [that] the drawing looks\" — a relation without marker between \"way\" and the clause. Plausible. And \"clear\" as modifier of c2 — settled: \"make the box red\": red is modifier since the box is complement. Here \"stay clear\" — \"clear\" is a complement of \"stay\" (adjective complement)... The settled case says \"make the box red\": \"red\" is a modifier of the clause since \"the box\" is a complement. So \"stay clear\" with no other complement — \"clear\" as modifier? By analogy, \"clear\" would be the complement of \"stay\" if nothing else is... but the circuit made \"just the way\" the complement and \"clear\" a modifier. Hmm. \"What a complement is to the verb is not judged\" — but which nominals are complements is judged (rule 5). \"stay clear\" — \"clear\" is an adjective, not a nominal; modifiers hold words that are neither nominals nor markers. \"clear\" is an adjective → modifier. Fine. And \"just the way\" as nominal complement of \"stay\"?? \"stay just the way the drawing looks\" — actually \"stay the way the drawing looks\" — \"the way...\" IS a complement of \"stay\" (stay [the way it looks]). OK, plausible. L8.\n\n**n=21**: \"for the order system, keep the associations labelled 1..*, then add a multiplicity note to the customer end, with enough space between the parts\"\n- r1 \"for\" from c1 to n1 (\"the order system\"). c1 imperative \"keep\" complement n2 (\"the associations\"). c2 nonfinite \"labelled\" complement n3 (\"1\")... and n4 \"*\" — hmm, k1? Let me re-read: c2 verb \"labelled\" complement=$n3; n3 \"1\"; n4 \"*\"; k1 \"then\" conjunct c1 c3. c3 imperative \"add\" complement n5 (\"a multiplicity note\"). r2 \"to\" from c3 to n6 (\"the customer end\"). r3 \"with\" from c3 to n7 (\"enough space\"). r4 \"between\" from n7 to n8 (\"the parts\"). r5 from n2 to c2. r6 from n2 to n4 (\"*\").\n- \"labelled 1..*\" — \"1..*\" — the circuit has n3 \"1\" as complement of c2, and n4 \"*\" tied from n2 (r6). Hmm — \"*\" tied to \"associations\" rather than to \"1\" or the labelled clause. \"1..*\" — punctuation stands in no wire; the words are \"1\" and \"*\". \"1\" complement of labelled, \"*\" tied to associations. The pairing \"1..*\" — per L6: \"A pairing the words give is not written: 'x 120' with '120' in another wire and tied to nothing\" — here \"*\" is tied (to n2). Hmm, tied to the wrong thing? \"labelled 1..*\" — the labels are \"1..*\" — \"*\" should be tied to \"1\" or to the labelled clause, not to \"associations\". Is that L3 (relation has wrong end)? Hmm. Or acceptable? The words \"1\" and \"*\" — \"1..*\" is a range. The circuit ties \"*\" to \"the associations\" — that says the associations are labelled \"*\"?? while \"1\" is the complement of \"labelled\". Splitting \"1..*\" into two wires with different ties... I think this is a plausible continuation? Eh — \"*\" tied to n2 says \"the associations *\" — hmm. It's L6-adjacent but it IS tied. I'd accept — the next layer can figure out \"1..*\" labels. Actually, is there a wrong statement? Not clearly. L8.\n- \"with enough space between the parts\" — r3 \"with\" from c3 to \"enough space\", r4 \"between\" from space to parts. Fine. L8.\n\n**n=22**: \"please est the Shell block into the upper right corner, three and a half widths in from the right and two lengths down from the top, next keep every other block as it is\"\n- c1 imperative, discourse \"please\", modifiers est/in/and/down/next, verb \"keep\", complements $n1 (the Shell block), $n3 (three and a half widths), $n5 (two length