— hmm. If \"est\" were a noun, \"est the Shell block\" would be weird. The pattern \"VERB the X into the Y\" strongly suggests verb. But it's misspelt, so maybe the formalizer couldn't know. Hmm. Compare n=12: \"est block 1 down to the bottom\" — there \"est\" precedes \"block 1\" without \"the\" — held as dependent of \"block\". Here \"est the Shell block\" — with \"the\" — \"est\" can't be part of the nominal \"the Shell block\" (est is before \"the\"). So \"est\" must be a verb or a separate nominal/modifier. Given \"into the upper right corner\" relation r1 from c1 — the circuit attached \"into\" to c1 (keep). The message says \"est the Shell block into the corner\" — the \"into\" relation belongs to \"est\", not \"keep\". The circuit states \"keep the Shell block into the upper right corner\" — wrong: the words say \"est ... into\". Hmm.\n- This is messy. What's the clearest wrong statement? The verb \"est\" held as modifier with no clause having it → L5. Or words of one clause held in another (L1: \"Words of one written clause are held in another\")? Hmm. The message's first command's verb is lost. I'll grade L5: \"'est' is the verb of the first command but is held as a modifier of the 'keep' clause, so no clause has it as verb\". Wait, but L5 says \"A clause of the message is not written: its verb is held as a modifier, a dependent, a head or a discourse word, and no clause has it as verb.\" Yes — \"est\" held as modifier, no clause has it as verb. But is \"est\" the verb of a clause of the message? If \"est\" is a misspelt \"set\", yes. Given \"please est the Shell block into...\" — I'm fairly confident it's a command verb. L5.\n- Hmm, alternatively the whole thing could be L1 (words of one clause held in another: \"est...into...\" held in the \"keep\" clause). L1 comes first. \"Words of one written clause are held in another\" — the circuit writes one clause; the message has two commands; the words of the first command (\"est\", \"into the upper right corner\") are held in the second's clause. Hmm, that's arguably L1. But the primary issue is the missing verb. The grades: L1 includes \"Words of one written clause are held in another.\" The message's first clause (est...) words are held in the keep clause. I could grade L1. But it's ambiguous whether \"est\" forms a clause. L5 is the safer, more precise: verb held as modifier, no clause has it. Actually, let me reconsider: is it possible the intended reading of the message is one command? \"please est the Shell block into the upper right corner, three and a half widths in from the right and two lengths down from the top, next keep every other block as it is\" — no, \"next keep every other block as it is\" is clearly a second command (\"next\" = then). So two commands; the circuit wrote one. The first command's verb \"est\" is a modifier → L5. I'll go L5. Note: \"'est' is held as a modifier of the 'keep' clause, so the command 'est the Shell block into the upper right corner' has no verb\".\n\n**n=23**: \"I keep squinting at Revenue trend every time I paste it into the weekly newsletter; this week, make the line medium width as well, would you. and then make the markers a bit bigger\"\n- c1 declarative \"keep\" subject I. c2 nonfinite \"squinting\" complement n3 (\"every time\"). r1 \"at\" from c2 to n2 (\"Revenue trend\"). r2 \"into\" from c3 to n6. n3 \"every time\" — r4 from n3 to c3 (paste clause: declarative \"paste\" subject I complement it). n7 \"this week\". c4 imperative \"make\", modifiers \"as\",\"well\", complements n7 (\"this week\"), n8 (\"the line medium width\"). c5 interrogative \"would\" subject \"you\". k1 \"and\" conjunct c1 c4 c6. c6 imperative modifier \"then\" verb \"make\" modifier \"bigger\" complements n10 (\"the markers\"), n11 (\"a bit\").\n- Issues: (a) \"this week\" as complement of \"make\" — \"this week, make the line...\" — \"this week\" is a time phrase; per settled case, a time set off by commas after a phrase... \"A day, an hour or a 