m. Written as interrogative clause with verb \"Let\" — per the settled case, \"let\" as verb of an imperative is right; interrogative is wrong → L1. Also c2 has complements n1 (\"us\") and n2 (\"what values\") — \"us\" as complement of \"show\" — weird. And \"what values does Bus show\" — c2 interrogative, subject \"Bus\", complement \"what values\" — should be \"what values\" as... in \"what values does Bus show?\", \"what values\" is the object (complement) of \"show\" — fine, or subject... \"does Bus show what values\" — \"what values\" is the direct object → complement. OK. But \"us\" as complement of \"show\" — wrong: \"us\" belongs to \"Let us\". Words of one clause held in another (L1). \n- Grade: L1. Note: \"'Let us' is a command, but 'Let' is written as an interrogative verb and 'us' is held as a complement of 'show'\". Good.\n\n**n=42**: \"could you put a little bench directly beneath the tree, with the fallen leaves around it.\"\n- c1 interrogative auxiliary \"could\" verb \"put\" modifier \"directly\" subject you complement n2 (a little bench). r1 \"beneath\" from c1 to n3 (the tree). r2 \"with\" from c1 to c2 (fragment complement n4 \"the fallen leaves\"). r3 \"around\" from n4 to n5 (\"it\").\n- \"with the fallen leaves around it\" — c2 fragment with complement n4, tied via r2 \"with\" from c1. r3 \"around\" from n4 to \"it\". Fine. L8.\n\n**n=43**: \"write a document with topic solar energy and title Quarterly plan and blocks one paragraph\"\n- c1 imperative \"write\" complement n1 (\"a document\"). r1 \"with\" from n1 to k1. n2 \"topic\", n3 \"solar energy\". k1 \"and\" conjunct n2, n4. n4 \"title\", n5 \"Quarterly plan\". k2 \"and\" conjunct c1, c2. c2 declarative verb \"blocks\" complement n6 (\"one paragraph\"). r2 from n2 to n3. r3 from n4 to n5.\n- \"and blocks one paragraph\" — \"blocks\" as a verb of a declarative clause c2 with complement \"one paragraph\" — \"blocks\" here is a noun (\"...and blocks [of] one paragraph\")? \"write a document with topic X and title Y and blocks one paragraph\" — \"blocks one paragraph\" — the document has blocks: one paragraph. \"blocks\" as verb declarative — \"the document blocks one paragraph\"?? That's a wrong statement? Or \"blocks\" as a noun (the blocks [are] one paragraph) — fragment. The circuit made c2 declarative with verb \"blocks\", subject none, complement \"one paragraph\". Hmm — \"blocks one paragraph\" — if \"blocks\" is a noun, then \"and blocks one paragraph\" = \"and [its] blocks [are] one paragraph\" — a fragment. The circuit's declarative clause \"blocks\" with complement — states that something blocks one paragraph — the message doesn't say that. L1 (verb is a word that is no verb here)? Or L3/L4? Per settled: \"A noun made its verb is L1\" (from the fragment case: \"A noun made its verb is L1\"). \"blocks\" here is a noun (list item: topic, title, blocks). Made the verb of a declarative clause → L1. Note: \"'blocks' is a noun in the list 'topic ... and title ... and blocks', but it is written as the verb of a declarative clause with 'one paragraph' as complement\". L1.\n\n**n=44**: \"the red block one place left and the blue block one place right, and after that, swap the red block with the blue block, then shift the pair one column left\"\n- n1 \"the red block\". n2 \"one place\". c1 fragment modifiers \"left\",\"right\" complements k2, n2, n4. k1 \"and\",\"and\",\"then\" conjunct c1, c2, c3. n3 \"the blue block\". n4 \"one place\". r1 \"after\" from c2 to n5 (\"that\"). c2 imperative \"swap\" complement n6 (\"the red block\"). r2 \"with\" from c2 to n7 (\"the blue block\"). c3 imperative \"shift\" modifier \"left\" complements n8 (\"the pair\"), n9 (\"one column\"). k2 coordination conjunct n1, n3.\n- Hmm: c1 fragment with complement k2 (the red block and the blue block), complements n2, n4 (one place each), modifiers left/right. So the first sentence: \"the red block one place left and the blue block one place right\" — as a fragment listing: red block + one place + left, blue block + one place + right. The circuit lumps both into one fragment c1 with modifiers left AND right — losing which block goes which way? Hmm — \"left\" and \"right\" both modifiers of c1 — the message says red goes left, blue goes right. The circuit holds both modifiers on one clause — the pairing lost? L6: \"a measure not tied to the relation it measures\" — \"one place left\" — \"left\" should tie to the red block's move... This is soft. \n- Also k1: \"and after that, swap ... then shift ...\" — the conjunctions \"and\" and \"then\": k1 has conjunction \"and\",\"and\",\"then\" with conjuncts c1, c2, c3. The message: \"X and Y, and after that, swap..., then shift...\" — the first \"and\" joins the red block part and the blue block part (k2), the second \"and\" (\"and after that\") joins the first sentence with \"swap\", \"then\" joins swap with shift. The circuit: k2 joins n1,n3 with \"and\" (the first and) — good. k1 joins c1 (the first-sentence fragment), c2 (swap), c3 (shift) with \"and\",\"and\",\"then\" — two \"and\"s and one \"then\" — but the message has \"and\" (between red/blue), \"and\" (after that), \"then\" (before shift). k1 uses \"and\" twice — but one of those \"and\"s is the red/blue one already used in k2! Each word stands in exactly one value — if k2 has conjunction \"and\" (the first and) and k1 has \"and\",\"and\",\"then\" — that's 2 more \"and\"s + \"then\" — the message has \"and\" (red and blue), \"and\" (and after that), \"then\" (then shift) — total two \"and\"s and one \"then\". k2 uses one \"and\", k1 uses \"and\",\"and\",\"then\" — that's three \"and\"s total but the message has only two! Wait, let me recount the message: \"the red block one place left and the blue block one place right, and after that, swap the red block with the blue block, then shift the pair one column left\" — \"and\" appears: (1) \"left and the blue\", (2) \"right, and after that\". \"then\" once. So two \"and\"s, one \"then\". The circuit: k2 conjunction=\"and\" (1), k1 conjunction=\"and\" conjunction=\"and\" conjunction=\"then\" (2 ands + then). Total: 3 \"and\"s. That's one too many — unless the program counts... hmm, maybe the checker allows repeated conjunction values? \"A program has already checked that every word of the message stands in exactly one value.\" If k1 lists \"and\" twice but the message has only two \"and\"s total and k2 has one, that's 3 — contradiction. Unless the checker counts occurrences... Maybe the checker is lenient. Whatever — the structure: k1 joins c1, c2, c3. The message's \"and after that\" joins the first part with \"swap\", and \"then\" joins swap with shift. So c1, c2, c3 in one coordination with \"and...then\" — plausible. \n- The \"left\"/\"right\" both as modifiers of c1 — the message: red block ... left, blue block ... right. The circuit: c1 has both left and right as modifiers, complements n2 (one place) and n4 (one place), and k2 (red and blue blocks). So the circuit says: [move] the red block and the blue block, one place, one place, left, right — losing which goes which way? Hmm, \"one place left\" for red and \"one place right\" for blue — the pairing of direction to block is lost. Is that \"lost explicit information\"? The settled case: \"move block 6 two spaces left\" — \"two spaces\" and \"left\"... In n=5, \"move block 7 three spaces left, then block 7 one space right\" — c1 modifier \"left\" with complement block 7 + three spaces; c2 modifier \"right\". There the directions were separated per clause. Here both in one clause — the circuit can't express which block gets which direction... but \"not judged: the order and names of wires\" — hmm. The information \"red goes left, blue goes right\" — in the circuit, c1 has complements {red block, blue block, one place, one place} and modifiers {left, right} — the next layer with context could... maybe not know which. Is this L6 (a pairing the words give is not written)? L6 mentions \"x 120\" pairing and measure-to-relation. The direction-block pairing — \"left\" tied to nothing specific. Hmm.\n- I'll accept L8 — plausible, not clearly wrong. Actually hmm, wait: is c1 even right? The first sentence \"the red block one place left and the blue block one place right\" — as a fragment with complements — fine, like n=12. L8.\n\n**n=45**: \"please label number n4 \\\"Risk\\\", cheers\"\n- c1 fragment discourse \"please\" complement n1 (\"label number\" — dependent label, head number). n2 \"n4\", n3 \"Risk\", n4 \"cheers\". r1 n1→n2, r2 n1→n3, r3 n1→n4.\n- \"please label number n4 \"Risk\"\" — \"label\" is a verb: \"label number n4 'Risk'\" — a command! The circuit made c1 a fragment with \"label\" as dependent of \"number\", and \"please\" as discourse. Per settled: \"A command needs its verb. When the first word of a message with no other verb can be a noun or a verb ('book design review', 'schedule the kickoff'), it is the verb; held as a noun in a fragment, the command is lost: L1.\" Here the message: \"please label number n4 \"Risk\", cheers\" — \"label\" is the verb (please + verb). The circuit holds \"label\" as dependent of \"number\" in a fragment → command lost → L1. Also \"cheers\" tied to n1 via r3 — \"cheers\" is a discourse word (\"thanks\") — held as a nominal tied by relation — hmm, L2 is content-in-discourse; here discourse-word-as-nominal — wrong wire L3? But the primary: L1 (command verb \"label\" held as dependent). Note: \"'label' is the verb of the command but is held as a dependent of 'number', so no clause has it as verb\". L1.\n\n**n=46**: \"quick question: how many undos until the row is back in the order it started with?\"\n- n1 \"quick question\". n2 \"how many undos\". r1 \"until\" from c3 to c1. n3 \"the row\". c1 declarative \"is\" modifier \"back\" subject n3. r2 \"in\" from c1 to n4 (\"the order\"). n5 \"it\". c2 declarative \"started\" subject n5. r3 \"with\" from c2 to n4. c3 fragment complement n1. r4 n1→n2. r5 n4→c2.\n- Structure: \"quick question: how many undos until the row is back in the order it started with?\" — c3 fragment \"quick question\", r1 \"until\" from c3 to c1 (\"the row is back...\"), r4 n1→n2 (\"how many undos\"). Hmm — \"how many undos until...\" — the \"until\" clause: \"until the row is back in the order it started with\" — r1 \"until\" from c3 to c1 — the until-clause attached to \"quick question\"?? \"how many undos until the row is back\" — the until clause belongs to \"how many undos\" (n2). The circuit attaches it to c3 (\"quick question\") — hmm, \"a relation has ... a start the words rule out\"? Not clearly ruled out — \"until\" could start at the fragment. Eh. Also \"how many undos\" — n2 with dependents how/many, head undos — \"how many\" as dependents — question word in nominal — fine. c1 \"is back in the order it started with\" — tied via r1 to c3, r2 to order, r5 order→c2. OK. L8 — messy but plausible. Hmm, one thing: the whole thing is a question (\"quick question: how many undos until...?\") — c3 is a fragment; the question is carried by \"how many undos\" nominal. The settled case: a clause that asks at the start of a sentence is interrogative — here no verb clause asks; \"how many undos until...\" is a fragment question. Fine. L8.\n\n**n=47**: \"how about thinner grid lines — while you're at it, flip the x axis over\"\n- c1 fragment modifier \"how\". r1 \"about\" from c1 to n1 (\"thinner grid lines\"). r2 \"while\" from c3 to c2. n2 \"you\". c2 declarative \"'re\" subject you. r3 \"at\" from c2 to n3 (\"it\"). c3 interrogative verb \"flip\" modifier \"over\" complement n4 (\"the x axis\"). k1 coordination conjunct c1, c3.\n- Issues: (a) c3 is interrogative — \"flip the x axis over\" after \"while you're at it,\" is a command, not a question. L1: \"a clear question written as imperative or declarative, or the reverse\" — here a command written as interrogative → L1. The settled case for the dash: \"— while you're at it, give it the title...\" — the relation \"while\" starts at the clause after the comma (\"give\"). Here r2 \"while\" from c3 (flip) to c2 — correct start. But c3's form: \"flip the x axis over\" is imperative (command), written interrogative → L1. Note: \"'flip the x axis over' is a command but is written as an interrogative clause\". L1.\n- (b) Also k1 coordination joins c1 (fragment \"how about thinner grid lines\") and c3 (imperative flip) — settled: \"A command or a question cannot stand inside another clause, so a coordination that joins an imperative or interrogative clause with such a clause has a wrong conjunct: L3.\" Hmm — that's about a coordination joining an imperative with a clause inside a relation (\"so it sits under the table\"). Here k1 joins c1 and c3 — c3 is interrogative... The settled case: \"In 'move the star so it sits under the table, then give it a thin outline', 'then' joins 'give' with 'move'.\" Here there's no conjunction word in k1 — the dash. Hmm. The main clear error is the interrogative form of a command. L1 first. Go L1.\n\n**n=48**: \"the dog on the lawn should face left, not right — with everything else left where it is — over on our little canvas\"\n- n1 \"the dog\", r1 \"on\" n1→n2 (the lawn). c1 declarative auxiliary \"should\" verb \"face\" modifier \"left\" discourse \"not\" modifier \"right\" modifier \"over\" subject n1. r2 \"with\" from c1 to n3 (\"everything else\"). c2 nonfinite \"left\". r3 \"where\" from c2 to c3 (\"is\" subject it). r4 \"on\" (ambiguous) from c1 to n5 (\"our little canvas\"). r5 from n3 to c2.\n- Issues: \"not right\" — \"not\" held as discourse! \"not\" is a negation word — L1: \"An auxiliary or negation word is held in another place, or negates the wrong clause.\" \"not\" held in discourse — the negation of \"face right\" is lost (the clause says face left, not right — the \"not\" should be negation of... hmm, \"should face left, not right\" — \"not right\" negates \"right\". The circuit: modifier \"left\", discourse \"not\", modifier \"right\" — so \"not\" as discourse loses the negation → the clause states \"face left ... right\"? The negation word \"not\" is held in discourse — L1. Note: \"'not' is a negation word but is held in discourse, so the negation of 'right' is lost\". L1.\n- Also \"over\" as modifier — \"over on our little canvas\" — \"over\" grading \"on\" — modifier of c1 — fine. L1 verdict.\n\n**n=49**: \"a big bench down at the bottom, then make a big sun in the top right corner, plus a tall tree on the left, then a small dog by the tree facing left\"\n- n1 \"a big bench\". r1 \"at\" from n1 to n2 (\"the bottom\"). k1 \"then\" conjunct c1, c2. c1 fragment modifiers \"down\",\"then\" complement k2. c2 imperative \"make\" complement n3 (\"a big sun\"). r2 \"in\" (ambiguous) from c2 to n4 (\"the top right corner\"). k2 \"plus\" conjunct n1, n5. n5 \"a tall tree\". r3 \"on\" from n5 to n6 (\"the left\"). n7 \"a small dog\". r4 \"by\" from n7 to n8 (\"the tree\"). c3 nonfinite \"facing\" modifier \"left\". r5 from n1 to n7. r6 from n7 to c3.\n- Structure: the message lists items: a big bench down at the bottom, then make a big sun in the top right corner, plus a tall tree on the left, then a small dog by the tree facing left. The circuit: c1 fragment (down, then) complement k2 = {bench, tree}; c2 make sun; k1 then joins c1, c2. The dog n7 tied from n1 (bench!) via r5 — \"then a small dog by the tree facing left\" — the dog tied to the bench?? r5 from $n1 to $n7 — the dog attached to \"a big bench\" — wrong end? The dog is a separate list item (\"then a small dog...\"), tied to the bench is wrong-ish. Hmm — but k2 (\"plus\") joins n1 (bench) and n5 (tree). The dog should be in that coordination too (\"then a small dog\") but instead r5 ties dog→bench. Hmm. Is the coordination missing the dog as conjunct? k2 = {bench, tree} with \"plus\" — the message has \"plus a tall tree\" — \"plus\" joins bench and tree. Then \"then a small dog\" — a third item joined by \"then\". The circuit ties the dog to the bench with a bare relation — that states the dog is related to the bench (like \"a bench with a dog\"?) — the message doesn't say that. L3 (relation the message does not relate → L4?) — L4: \"a relation or a coordination between wires that the message does not relate\". Hmm, \"then a small dog by the tree\" — the dog is introduced by \"then\" — the circuit's r5 dog→bench... eh. Or is it acceptable — the dog tied to the bench (the first item) as a list continuation? The settled case: \"The earlier wire a coordination joins...\" — hmm.\n- Also c1 fragment has modifiers \"down\",\"then\" and complement k2 — \"a big bench down at the bottom ... then ...\" — c1 is the fragment for the first part; \"then\" held as modifier of c1 — \"then\" is a conjunction word held as modifier — per settled: \"'And', 'Then' or 'So' at the start of a message, with nothing before it to join, is a discourse word\" — here \"then\" is mid-message joining parts. Held as modifier of c1 — hmm, L3 \"a linking word is held as a modifier\"? \"then\" is a conjunction, not a marker. \"So is a conjunction between two modifier words of one clause ('flat and dry')\" — discourse. Here \"then\" between \"down at the bottom\" and \"make a big sun\" — it joins the parts — should be a coordination conjunction. The circuit has k1 \"then\" joining c1 and c2 — wait, k1 conjunction=\"then\" — so one \"then\" is the conjunction of k1. And c1 also has modifier \"then\" — two \"then\"s in the message: \"..., then make a big sun ..., plus ..., then a small dog ...\" — yes two \"then\"s. k1 uses one, c1's modifier uses the other. Hmm — the second \"then\" (\"then a small dog\") is held as modifier of c1 — but c1 is the first fragment; the second \"then\" introduces the dog. Holding it as modifier of c1 — wrong-ish. And the dog tied to bench.\n- This is messy. Clearest error: r5 ties \"a small dog\" to \"a big bench\" — the message does not relate the dog to the bench (\"then a small dog by the tree facing left\" — relates dog to the tree via \"by\"). L4: \"a relation ... between wires that the message does not relate\" — the dog-bench relation is not in the message. Hmm, but L4 is about stating what the message does not say. A bare relation dog→bench states they're related — the message doesn't say that. L4? Or L3 (wrong end)? The message relates the dog via \"then\" to the list — a coordination. The circuit uses a relation instead. \"A pairing the words give is not written\" — L6? Ugh.\n- Alternatively accept: the dog is tied into the sentence (not untied), the next layer can continue. The instruction says \"reject only a wrong statement or lost explicit information\". Is dog→bench a wrong statement? It states the dog stands in some relation to the bench — the message says \"then a small dog by the tree\" — the dog's relation is to the tree. The circuit DOES have r4 \"by\" from dog to the tree. So the dog has both: by→tree and →bench. The extra bench relation is a wrong statement (message doesn't relate dog and bench). L4. Hmm, or is it harmless? \"a relation or a coordination between wires that the message does not relate\" — L4 exactly. I'll grade L4. Note: \"the relation from 'a small dog' to 'a big bench' is not in the words, which say 'then a small dog by the tree'\". Hmm, wait — but maybe the intended reading: \"then\" chains: bench, then sun, plus tree, then dog — the dog relates to the sequence... The settled case: \"Which earlier wire a coordination joins, when grammar allows several: 'profit by ship mode and customer segment'.\" The circuit could have made the dog a conjunct of a coordination. Instead it's a relation from the bench. I'll go L4. Hmm, actually, let me reconsider — L6 says \"A clause or a nominal is tied to nothing inside its sentence\" — the dog IS tied. L4 says relations not in the message. The dog-bench relation: the words \"then a small dog\" — \"then\" implies sequence with what precedes — a relation to the preceding wire is arguably given by \"then\"... \"then\" is a linking word. Eh. I could accept. But \"then\" was already used as modifier of c1... \n- Decision: L8? or L4? Let me think about what a careful judge would say. The message: four items to add, introduced by \"then\"/\"plus\"/\"then\". The circuit: bench and tree in a \"plus\" coordination; dog tied to bench by a bare relation; sun in a \"make\" clause joined by \"then\" to the fragment c1. The information \"a small dog\" is present and tied (to bench and by-tree and facing). Nothing explicit is lost. The bench-dog relation is an extra statement not in the message — L4 technically. But is it \"a relation between wires that the message does not relate\"? The message's \"then a small dog\" — sequence — relates the dog to what came before. I'll accept L8. Hmm... I keep flip-flopping. The guidance: \"Judge fairly: a plausible or incomplete reading that the next layer can continue from is acceptable; reject only a wrong statement or lost explicit information.\" The dog→bench relation is a mild wrong statement but the reading is plausible (the dog is part of the bench scene?). I'll go L8.\n\n**n=50**: \"make part 0 of House A purple\"\n- c1 imperative \"make\" modifier \"purple\" complement n1 (\"part\"). n2 \"0\". r1 \"of\" from n1 to n3 (\"House A\"). r2 from n1 to n2.\n- \"part 0 of House A\" — n1 \"part\", n2 \"0\" tied from n1 (number after noun — passes), r1 \"of\" from n1 to \"House A\" — \"of\" after a nominal starts at that nominal — correct. \"purple\" modifier of clause (make X purple — settled). L8.\n\n**n=51**: same as n=3 with \"nope\" — L8 (same analysis). \"nope\" as discourse — fine.\n\n**n=52**: \"Can you point at the region with the highest revenue, and how large a share is it?\"\n- c1 interrogative auxiliary \"Can\" verb \"point\" subject you. r1 \"at\" from c1 to n2 (\"the region\"). r2 \"with\" from n2 to n3 (\"the highest revenue\"). k1 \"and\" conjunct c1, c2. n4 \"how large a share\" (dependents how/large/a, head share). c2 interrogative verb \"is\" subject n5 (\"it\") complement n4.\n- \"how large a share is it?\" — c2 interrogative, subject \"it\", complement \"how large a share\" — \"how large a share\" as complement of \"is\" — in \"how large a share is it?\", \"how large a share\" is the predicate... complement of \"is\" — fine. L8.\n\n**n=53**: \"replace Bus driver note with \\\"Bring the revised notes to the meeting.\\\".\"\n- c1 imperative \"replace\" complement n1 (\"Bus driver note\"). r1 \"with\" (ambiguous) from c1 to n2 (the quoted sentence as nominal head).\n- Quoted content as nominal — fine. L8.\n\n**n=54**: \"a little cat near the bench, then redo the house roof as one of those barn roofs, nothing else of the house, everything else untouched\"\n- n1 \"a little cat\". r1 \"near\" from n1 to n2 (the bench). c1 fragment modifier \"then\" complement n1. c2 imperative \"redo\" complement n3 (\"the house roof\"). r2 \"as\" from c2 to n4 (\"one\"). r3 \"of\" from n4 to n5 (\"those barn roofs\"). n6: head \"nothing\" with dependents else/everything/else/untouched — \"nothing else of the house, everything else untouched\" merged into ONE nominal with head \"nothing\" and dependents \"else\",\"everything\",\"else\",\"untouched\"! r4 \"of\" from n6 to n7 (\"the house\"). k1 coordination c1, c2. r5 from n4 to n6.\n- Issues: (a) \"nothing else of the house, everything else untouched\" — two phrases merged into one nominal (head \"nothing\", dependents else, everything, else, untouched) — \"two nominals are merged\" → L3. Also \"else\" — settled: \"'everything else', 'something else', 'anyone else': one nominal, with 'else' a dependent of the pronoun. 'else' held as a modifier of a clause splits the nominal: L3.\" Here \"else\" is a dependent — fine per that case, but the two phrases are merged. (b) r5 from n4 (\"one\") to n6 (the merged nothing/everything nominal) — \"one of those barn roofs, nothing else...\" — the \"nothing else\" relates to the redo, not to \"one\". Eh.\n- The clear error: \"nothing else of the house, everything else untouched\" merged into one nominal — two nominals merged → L3. Note: \"'nothing else of the house' and 'everything else untouched' are merged into one nominal with head 'nothing'\". L3.\n\n**n=55**: \"first the annotation sweep, then the underline on sections Intro, 2, 3 and Second, enabled marker Main, attrs in tow\"\n- c1 fragment modifiers \"first\",\"then\" complement n1 (\"the annotation sweep\"). n2 \"the underline\". r1 \"on\" from n2 to k1. n3 \"sections Intro\" (dependent sections, head Intro). n4 \"2\", n5 \"3\". k1 \"and\" conjunct n3, n4, n5, n6. n6 \"Second\". n7 \"enabled marker Main\" (dependents enabled/marker, head Main). n8 \"attrs\". r2 \"in\" from n8 to n9 (\"tow\"). r3 from n1 to n7. r4 from n1 to n8. r5 from n1 to n2.\n- \"first the annotation sweep, then the underline on sections Intro, 2, 3 and Second, enabled marker Main, attrs in tow\" — the circuit ties: annotation sweep → underline (r5), → \"enabled marker Main\" (r3), → attrs (r4). The \"then\" held as modifier of c1; \"first\" as modifier. k1 \"and\" joins Intro, 2, 3, Second — the sections list — good. \"enabled marker Main\" as a nominal tied from n1 — \"enabled marker Main\" — hmm, \"enabled marker Main\" = the enabled marker is Main. As a nominal (head Main) tied to the sweep — plausible. \"attrs in tow\" — r2 \"in\" from attrs to \"tow\" — \"in tow\" fixed phrase — \"in\" relation to \"tow\" — eh, plausible (\"attrs in tow\" — attrs with \"in tow\"). L8 — messy but nothing clearly wrong. Accept L8.\n\n**n=56**: \"draw a bird above the pond so the whole scene still reads — keeping the rest of the scene as it is\"\n- c1 imperative \"draw\" complement n1 (\"a bird\"). r1 \"above\" from c1 to n2 (\"the pond\"). r2 \"so\" from c1 to c2. n3 \"the whole scene\". c2 declarative modifier \"still\" verb \"reads\" subject n3. c3 nonfinite \"keeping\" complement n4 (\"the rest\"). r3 \"of\" from n4 to n5 (\"the scene\"). r4 \"as\" from c3 to c4 (\"is\" subject it). r5 from c1 to c3.\n- Fine. \"keeping the rest of the scene as it is\" tied to c1 via r5. L8.\n\n**n=57**: \"scatter plot of for each region scatter: sales versus profit, with gridlines behind it, legend at the bottom, as the closing chart for the review, and show the values on it\"\n- n1 \"scatter plot\". c1 fragment modifier \"of\" complement n1, n3. r1 \"for\" from n1 to n2 (\"each region\"). n3 \"scatter\". n4 \"sales\", r2 \"versus\" from n4 to n5 (\"profit\"). r3 \"with\" from n4 to n6 (\"gridlines\"). r4 \"behind\" from n6 to n7 (\"it\"). n8 \"legend\". r5 \"at\" from n8 to n9 (\"the bottom\"). r6 \"as\" from n8 to n10 (\"the closing chart\"). r7 \"for\" from n10 to n11 (\"the review\"). k1 \"and\" conjunct c1, c2. c2 imperative \"show\" complement n12 (\"the values\"). r8 \"on\" from n12 to n13 (\"it\"). r9 from n3 to n4. r10 from n4 to n8.\n- Messy but: \"scatter plot of for each region scatter: sales versus profit...\" — the circuit ties things around. \"of\" as modifier of c1 — \"plot of\" — \"of\" held as modifier?? L3: \"A linking word is held as a modifier or a dependent.\" Here c1 has modifier=\"of\" — \"of\" is a linking word (preposition) held as a modifier of the fragment! That's L3 per the rule. Hmm — wait: \"scatter plot of for each region scatter\" — the \"of\" links \"plot\" to \"for each region scatter\". The circuit made c1 fragment with modifier \"of\" and complement n1 (\"scatter plot\") and n3 (\"scatter\")... and r1 \"for\" from n1 to \"each region\". So \"of\" is held as a modifier — L3: \"A linking word is held as a modifier or a dependent.\" Note: \"'of' is a linking word but is held as a modifier of the fragment\". L3. Hmm, but is \"of\" here really a marker that should have a relation? \"scatter plot of X\" — yes, \"of\" governs the complement. The circuit instead: complement n3 \"scatter\" directly (no marker), and \"of\" as modifier. That's a linking word as modifier → L3. \n- Also \"with gridlines behind it\" — r3 \"with\" from n4 (sales) to gridlines — \"with\" should attach to the clause/fragment, not \"sales\". Wrong end? \"The start of a relation with a marker: the clause, or any nominal of that clause that grammar allows\" — \"with gridlines behind it\" — attaches to the whole thing, not to \"sales\" specifically. Hmm, \"sales versus profit, with gridlines behind it\" — \"with\" could attach to \"profit\" or the clause... grammar allows several? Eh.\n- The clearest: \"of\" as modifier → L3. Go L3. Note quotes 'of' and 'scatter plot'. \"In 'scatter plot of', the linking word 'of' is held as a modifier of the fragment instead of marking the relation to what the plot is of\". L3.\n\n**n=58**: \"to the right of the main road, there should be a round well, on the side whre the sun sets.\"\n- r1 \"to\" from c1 to n1 (\"the right\"). r2 \"of\" from n1 to n2 (\"the main road\"). n3 \"there\". c1 declarative auxiliary \"should\" verb \"be\" subject n3 complement n4 (\"a round well\"). r3 \"on\" from c1 to n5 (\"the side\"). r4 \"whre\" from n5 to c2 (\"sets\" subject \"the sun\").\n- \"to the right of the main road\" — r1 \"to\" from c1 to \"the right\", r2 \"of\" from \"the right\" to \"the main road\" — per settled: \"'left of the hill', 'right of the road': 'left of' may be one marker, or 'left' a nominal or clause modifier with the relation marked 'of' starting at the clause or at 'left'.\" Here: \"right\" as nominal, r1 \"to\" from c1 to right?? Hmm — \"to the right of the main road\" — the circuit has r1 marker \"to\" from c1 to n1 (\"the right\") — \"to\" as a marker — \"to the right\" — and then \"of\" from right to road. The settled case allows \"left of\" as one marker, or \"left\" as nominal with \"of\" relation starting at clause or \"left\". Here there's an extra \"to\" relation from the clause to \"right\" — \"to the right of the main road\" — the whole phrase is a place modifier. The circuit: relation \"to\" from c1 to \"the right\", relation \"of\" from \"the right\" to \"the main road\" — decomposition of \"to the right of X\" — plausible. \"whre\" (misspelt \"where\") as marker from \"the side\" to c2 — \"on the side where the sun sets\" — \"whre\" held as marker of relation — a misspelt word as marker — \"held where it stands\" — plausible. L8.\n\n**n=59**: \"a little black cat curled up near the bench, then set the fence solidly bold mustard, right over the whole of it\"\n- n1 \"a little black cat\". c1 declarative \"curled\" modifier \"up\" subject n1. r1 \"near\" from c1 to n2 (\"the bench\"). k1 \"then\" conjunct c1, c2. c2 imperative \"set\" modifier \"right\" complements n3 (\"the fence\"), n4 (\"solidly bold mustard\" — dependents solidly/bold, head mustard). r2 \"over\" from c2 to n5 (\"the whole\"). r3 \"of\" from n5 to n6 (\"it\").\n- \"set the fence solidly bold mustard\" — n4: head \"mustard\", dependents \"solidly\",\"bold\" — \"mustard\" as head of the color nominal — \"set the fence mustard\" with \"solidly bold\" as dependents of \"mustard\"?? Hmm — \"solidly\" and \"bold\" describe how the fence is set (modifiers), \"mustard\" is the color (complement-ish). Per settled: \"make the box red\": \"red\" is a modifier of the clause. Here \"mustard\" as head of a nominal complement, with \"solidly\",\"bold\" as its dependents — the grading words \"solidly\",\"bold\"... hmm, they don't grade \"mustard\"; they're manner modifiers. Held as dependents of the \"mustard\" nominal — \"solidly bold mustard\" as a color description? Eh. Per L3, words in wrong wire — \"solidly\",\"bold\" should be modifiers of the clause. Hmm, but they could describe the mustard color (\"solidly bold mustard\" = a solid bold mustard color). Plausible! \"right over the whole of it\" — r2 \"over\" from c2 to \"the whole\", r3 \"of\" from whole to \"it\" — \"over the whole of it\" — fine; \"right\" grading \"over\" as modifier of c2 — fine. L8.\n\n**n=60**: \"mouth — add a box, please\"\n- n1 \"mouth\". c1 imperative \"add\" discourse \"please\" complement n2 (\"a box\"). c2 fragment complement n1. k1 coordination conjunct c2, c1 (no conjunction — the dash).\n- \"mouth — add a box\" — \"mouth\" as a fragment tied via coordination to the command. Plausible (like a label). L8.\n\n**n=61**: \"Main is who the Intro meeting should carry as participants\"\n- n1 \"Main\". c1 declarative \"is\" subject n1. n2 \"who\". n3 \"the Intro meeting\". c2 declarative auxiliary \"should\" verb \"carry\" subject n3 complement n2. r1 \"as\" from c2 to n4 (\"participants\"). r2 from c1 to c2.\n- \"Main is who the meeting should carry as participants\" — \"who\" as complement of \"carry\" — \"carry who as participants\" — \"who\" is the object of carry — fine. r2 ties c1 to c2. L8.\n\n**n=62**: \"turn bold on for characters 10 to 15 of Body\"\n- c1 imperative \"turn\" modifiers \"bold\",\"on\". r1 \"for\" from c1 to n1 (\"characters\"). n2 \"10\". n3 \"to 15\" (dependent to, head 15) — \"to\" held as dependent of \"15\"! L3: \"A linking word is held as a modifier or a dependent.\" \"10 to 15\" — \"to\" is a linking word between 10 and 15 — held as dependent of the nominal \"15\" → L3. Hmm — or \"to\" could be... \"characters 10 to 15\" — the \"to\" links 10 and 15. The circuit: r3 from n1 to n2 (characters→10), r4 from n2 to n3 (10→\"to 15\"). So the relation from 10 to the nominal \"to 15\" (head 15, dependent \"to\"). \"to\" as dependent — wrong wire → L3. Alternatively \"10 to 15\" could be one nominal... The settled cases: \"a number or a name right after a noun may be a dependent in that noun's nominal, or a nominal of its own tied by a relation without a marker\" — \"10 to 15\": \"to\" is a marker; \"characters 10 to 15 of Body\" — the relation \"to\" from 10 to 15 with marker \"to\", or \"to\" in the marker of a relation. Held as dependent → L3. Note: \"'to' in '10 to 15' is a linking word but is held as a dependent of '15'\". L3.\n- Also r2 \"of\" from n1 to n4 (\"Body\") — \"characters ... of Body\" — fine. L3 verdict.\n\n**n=63**: \"a pointed for me, ear olive\"\n- n1 head \"a\". c1 fragment modifier \"pointed\" complement n1. r1 \"for\" from c1 to n2 (\"me\"). n3 \"ear olive\" (dependent ear, head olive). r2 from n1 to n3.\n- \"a pointed for me, ear olive\" — garbled (like n=4). \"a\" as head of nominal, \"pointed\" modifier, \"ear olive\" nominal (head olive, dependent ear) tied to \"a\". Hmm — \"ear olive\" — \"ear\" dependent of \"olive\" — two bare nouns side by side (\"with id neck\", \"type radar\"): either may be the head — passes. \"a\" as head — the article as head — eh. n=4 similar. Accept L8.\n\n**n=64**: \"and Claims by month on the revenue trend needs the same yellow fading to red, please\"\n- c1 declarative discourse \"and\" verb \"needs\" discourse \"please\" subject n1 (\"Claims\"). r1 \"by\" from n1 to n2 (\"month\"). r2 \"on\" (ambiguous) from c1 to n3 (\"the revenue trend\"). n4 \"the same yellow\". c2 nonfinite \"fading\" subject n4. r3 \"to\" from c2 to n5 (\"red\"). r4 from c1 to c2.\n- \"and\" at start as discourse — settled, passes. \"please\" discourse — fine. \"the same yellow fading to red\" — c2 nonfinite fading with subject \"the same yellow\", tied to c1 via r4. L8.\n\n**n=65**: \"reschedule Review, 2026-11-18 at 10:00 UTC\"\n- c1 imperative \"reschedule\" complement n1 (\"Review\"). n2 \"2026-11-18\". r1 \"at\" (ambiguous) from c1 to n3 (\"10:00 UTC\"). r2 from n1 to n2.\n- \"reschedule Review, 2026-11-18 at 10:00 UTC\" — the date 2026-11-18 tied to \"Review\" (r2), and \"at 10:00 UTC\" from c1. Per the time settled case: \"A day, an hour or a length of time set off by commas after a phrase with a marker ('book the sync at 16:00, Friday, for 60 minutes') belongs to the clause: a relation from the clause with no marker passes, and so does a coordination with the time before it.\" Here \"2026-11-18\" (a day set off by a comma) is tied to \"Review\" (r2 from n1) instead of the clause — hmm. The case says it \"belongs to the clause: a relation from the clause with no marker passes, and so does a coordination with the time before it. A coordination with what the verb acts on states a second thing acted on: L3.\" Here r2 is a relation from \"Review\" (what the verb acts on) to the date — not a coordination, a relation. Does that \"state a second thing acted on\"? A relation from Review to the date — states the date is tied to Review — hmm. The failure in the settled case was a coordination with the acted-on thing. A relation from the clause passes; here the relation is from the nominal \"Review\" — the words... \"reschedule Review, 2026-11-18 at 10:00 UTC\" — the date modifies the rescheduling, could be tied to Review (\"Review [on] 2026-11-18\") — plausible. Accept. L8.\n\n**n=66**: \"get the dog on the lawn to chase the cat, and leave everything else in the picture untouched — the way the drawing looks so far\"\n- c1 imperative \"get\" complement n1 (\"the dog\"). r1 \"on\" (ambiguous) from c1 to n2 (\"the lawn\"). r2 \"to\" from c1 to c2 (nonfinite \"chase\" complement n3 \"the cat\"). k1 \"and\" conjunct c1, c3. c3 imperative \"leave\" modifier \"untouched\" complements n4 (\"everything else\"), n6 (\"the way\"). r3 \"in\" from n4 to n5 (\"the picture\"). n7 \"the drawing\". c4 declarative \"looks\" modifiers \"so\",\"far\" subject n7. r4 from n6 to c4.\n- \"— the way the drawing looks so far\" — n6 \"the way\" as complement of \"leave\"!! \"leave everything else in the picture untouched — the way the drawing looks so far\" — \"the way...\" is an appositive/comparison to \"untouched\", not something left. Held as a second complement of \"leave\" — states \"leave the way the drawing looks so far\" — a second thing acted on — wrong. Per the time-phrase settled case analog: \"A coordination with what the verb acts on states a second thing acted on: L3\" — here a second complement. L3: \"leave ... 'the way' as a complement states the verb acts on 'the way the drawing looks so far'\". Hmm — or L3 \"a nominal is the complement of the wrong clause\"? It's the right clause but wrong role. The words: \"leave everything else in the picture untouched — the way the drawing looks so far\" — \"the way...\" explains how things are left (as the drawing looks). Held as complement of leave — wrong statement. L3. Note: \"'the way the drawing looks so far' is held as a complement of 'leave', but the words only compare the leaving to 'the way the drawing looks so far'\". L3.\n\n**n=67**: \"for the monthly ops review on Friday I need the revenue trend legible from the back of the conference room, so how about line thinner?; also let's have the marker borders thicker on the revenue trend before we circulate it wider; plus show both grid lines\"\n- r1 \"for\" from c1 to n1 (\"the monthly ops review\"). r2 \"on\" from n1 to n2 (\"Friday\"). n3 \"I\". c1 interrogative verb \"need\" modifier \"legible\" subject n3 complement n4 (\"the revenue trend\"). Hmm — c1 is interrogative?! \"I need the revenue trend legible...\" — declarative! \"for the monthly ops review on Friday I need...\" — a statement. Written as interrogative → L1. Note: \"'I need the revenue trend legible' is a statement but is written as an interrogative clause\". L1.\n- (Rest: r3 \"from\" c1→back; k1 \"so\",\"plus\" conjunct c1, c2, c3, c6; c2 fragment \"how about line thinner\" ellipsis c1 — \"so how about line thinner?\" — fragment with ellipsis to c1 (need)?? \"how about line thinner\" — ellipsis=$c1 — sharing the verb \"need\"?? \"so [I need] line thinner\" — eh, plausible. c3 imperative \"let\" complement \"'s\"; c4 nonfinite \"have\"... etc.) The clear error: c1 form interrogative for a declarative statement. L1.\n\n**n=68**: \"same as last step exept with objects 3 and 4\"\n- c1 fragment modifier \"same\". r1 \"as\" from c1 to n1 (\"last step\"). n2 \"exept\". r2 \"with\" from n2 to k1. n3 \"objects\". n4 \"3\". k1 \"and\" conjunct n3, n5. n5 \"4\". r3 from n1 to n2. r4 from n3 to n4.\n- \"same as last step exept with objects 3 and 4\" — \"exept\" (misspelt \"except\") as a nominal head with \"with\" relation from it to the objects coordination — \"except with objects 3 and 4\" — \"except\" as a marker would be better, but \"exept\" misspelt held as a nominal... \"A misspelt or unknown word, held where it stands, when the words do not settle what it is\" — the words settle: \"except with objects\" — \"except\" is a preposition. Held as head of nominal n2 with r2 \"with\" from it — hmm, that reads \"last step exept-with objects\" — the relation r3 from n1 to n2 ties \"last step\" to \"exept\". Plausible-ish? \"same as last step except with objects 3 and 4\" — the circuit: c1 \"same\", r1 \"as\" c1→last step, r3 last step→exept (bare), r2 \"with\" exept→objects 3 and 4. Eh — \"exept\" as a nominal is weird but the words... \"except with\" — could be one marker \"except with\" (like n=78 \"except with\" as one marker!). In n=78: r2 marker=\"except with\" from n1 to n4. Here split with \"exept\" as nominal. Since \"exept\" is misspelt, \"held where it stands\" — as head of a nominal — the words don't settle what it is (misspelt) → passes? I'll accept L8.\n\n**n=69**: \"perhaps you could seat a green block in the middle of the top row and in the second column of red blocks\"\n- c1 declarative modifier \"perhaps\" auxiliary \"could\" verb \"seat\" subject you complement n2 (\"a green block\"). r1 \"in\" from c1 to n3 (\"the middle\"). r2 \"of\" from n3 to n4 (\"the top row\"). k1 \"and\" conjunct r1, r3. r3 \"in\" from c1 to n5 (\"the second column\"). r4 \"of\" from n5 to n6 (\"red blocks\").\n- \"in the middle of the top row and in the second column of red blocks\" — coordination of the two \"in\" relations — k1 \"and\" conjunct $r1, $r3 — coordinating relations — allowed? \"Which earlier wire a coordination joins, when grammar allows several\" — coordinating two relations — plausible. L8.\n\n**n=70**: \"draw a small sun partly hidden behind the tree, not in front of it.\"\n- c1 imperative \"draw\" complement n1 (\"a small sun\"). c2 nonfinite modifier \"partly\" verb \"hidden\". r1 \"behind\" from c2 to n2 (\"the tree\"). r2 negation \"not\" marker \"in\" from c2 to n3 (\"front\"). r3 \"of\" from n3 to n4 (\"it\"). r4 from n1 to c2.\n- \"not in front of it\" — r2 has negation=\"not\" marker \"in\" — good. L8.\n\n**n=71**: \"put a box between Draft ant Review\"\n- c1 imperative \"put\" complement n1 (\"a box\"). r1 \"between\" (ambiguous) from c1 to n2 (\"Draft ant Review\" — dependents Draft/ant, head Review).\n- \"between Draft ant Review\" — \"ant\" = \"and\" misspelt? \"between Draft and Review\" — the circuit made \"Draft ant Review\" one nominal with head \"Review\". Hmm — \"between Draft and Review\" — \"between A and B\" — the coordination of Draft and Review under \"between\". The circuit merged \"Draft ant Review\" into one nominal — \"two bare nouns side by side after a marker ('with id neck', 'type radar'): either may be the head\" — that's for two nouns; here three words \"Draft ant Review\" where \"ant\" is presumably \"and\". Merging into one nominal — the \"and\" lost? If \"ant\" is \"and\", the message relates Draft and Review by coordination; the circuit merged them into one nominal — lost the coordination → hmm. But \"ant\" is misspelt/unknown — \"held where it stands, when the words do not settle what it is\". The words: \"between Draft ant Review\" — \"ant\" unknown — held as dependent — passes per the misspelt-word case. L8.\n\n**n=72**: \"let's have the font smaler on the profit chart and then set the error bar cap size smaller, that's the tweak\"\n- c1 imperative verb \"let\" complement n1 (\"'s\"). c2 nonfinite \"have\". c3 nonfinite modifier \"the\" verb \"smaler\" complement n2 (\"font\"). Hmm — c3: verb \"smaler\" (misspelt \"smaller\") as the VERB of a nonfinite clause! \"have the font smaler on the profit chart\" — \"smaler\" is an adjective (complement/adjective of \"have the font\"), not a ve