the reverse\" refers to question/statement reversal. A verbless part written as a clause with the adjective as verb — that's \"Its verb is a word that is no verb here\"? \"hollow\" is a verb in the language... Ugh.\n- Let me compare with n=116 where \"rides\", \"repairs\", \"spares\" are clearly nouns made verbs → L1 per the settled fragment case (\"A noun made its verb is L1\"). In n=93, \"hollow\" is an adjective primarily but also a verb. The message: \"the inheritance arrows hollow\" — parallel to \"pie radius smaller\" (fragment with modifier). So \"hollow\" should be a modifier of a fragment; made the verb of a declarative clause → per the settled case pattern (noun made verb is L1), adjective made verb similarly L1. I'll grade L1: \"'hollow' in 'the inheritance arrows hollow' is the adjective the arrows should be, but it is written as the verb of a declarative clause\". Hmm, but \"hollow\" as verb is grammatically possible... The settled case says \"A noun made its verb is L1\" — for nouns. For \"hollow\" — \"make the box red\" case says \"red\" is a modifier — by analogy \"hollow\" is a modifier. Written as verb → wrong. L1.\n- Continue n=93: k1 \"plus\" conjunct c1, c2, c3. c3 imperative \"mark\" complement n3 (\"the parent class\"). r1 \"at\" from c3 to n4 (\"the arrow tail\"). r2 \"so\" from c3 to c4 (\"fits\" subject it, modifier still). r3 \"on\" from c4 to n6 (\"a single slide\"). r4 \"without\" from c4 to c5 (nonfinite \"crowding\").\n- \"plus mark the parent class at the arrow tail\" — \"plus\" joins c1, c2, c3 — the message: \"here is the plan: the arrows hollow, plus mark the parent class..., so it still fits...\" — \"plus\" joins \"hollow\" and \"mark\" — and c1 (\"here is the plan\")? k1 includes c1 — \"plus\" joining \"here is the plan\" — the wire before \"plus\" is \"hollow\" (c2)... The coordination includes c1 — c1 is separated by the colon. Hmm — \"the earlier wire a coordination joins is the wire that ends right before the conjunction, or 