4 and then a parallel chart for 2023 on a shared y axis and then give the first chart the title 'Claims by region, two years'\"\n- c1 fragment modifier \"How\". r1 \"about\" from c1 to k2. n1 \"a column chart\". r2 \"of\" from n1 to n2 (\"claims\"). r3 \"by\" from n2 to n3 (\"region\"). r4 \"for\" from n1 to n4 (\"2024\"). k1 \"and\",\"and\" conjunct c1, c2. n5 \"then a parallel chart\" (dependent then, head chart — \"then\" as dependent of the nominal!). c2 imperative modifier \"then\" verb \"give\" complements n8, n9. r5 \"for\" from n5 to n6 (\"2023\"). r6 \"on\" from n5 to n7 (\"a shared y axis\"). k2 coordination conjunct n1, n5. r7 from n9 to n10 (\"Claims by region, two years\").\n- \"then a parallel chart\" — \"then\" as dependent of \"a parallel chart\" (n5) — \"then\" is a word of order — held as dependent → L3 (per the settled case: \"A word of time or order cannot describe a noun from where it stands... it is a modifier of the clause; held as a dependent, it fails: L3\"). Same as n=90. L3. Note: \"'then' in 'then a parallel chart' is a word of order but is held as a dependent of 'chart'\". L3.\n\n**n=100**: \"make the avatar a guy, human\"\n- c1 imperative \"make\" complements n1 (\"the avatar\"), n2 (\"a guy\"). n3 \"human\". r1 from n2 to n3.\n- \"human\" tied to \"a guy\" — appositive — fine. L8.\n\n**n=101**: \"just undo the latest, whatever it was\"\n- c1 imperative modifier \"just\" verb \"undo\" complement n1 (\"the latest\"). r1 \"whatever\" from n1 to c2 (\"was\" subject \"it\").\n- \"whatever it was\" — r1 marker \"whatever\" from \"the latest\" to c2 — \"the latest, whatever it was\" — the relation \"whatever\" — hmm, \"whatever\" as a marker linking \"the latest\" to the clause \"it was\" — plausible (like \"where\" in n=48). L8.\n\n**n=102**: \"could you place a tree twice as tall as the boy — the way the drawing looks so far — exactly where the picture needs it\"\n- c1 interrogative auxiliary \"could\" verb \"place\" modifiers twice/as/tall/exactly subject you complement n2 (\"a tree\"). r1 \"as\" from c1 to n3 (\"the boy\"). n4 \"the way\". n5 \"the drawing\". c2 declarative \"looks\" modifiers so/far subject n5. r2 \"where\" from c1 to c3 (\"needs\" subject \"the picture\" complement \"it\"). r3 from n2 to n4. r4 from n4 to c2.\n- \"twice as tall as the boy\" — modifiers twice/as/tall of c1, r1 \"as\" from c1 to the boy — hmm, \"as\" appears twice: \"twice as tall as the boy\" — one \"as\" as modifier, one as marker of r1. OK. \"the way the drawing looks so far\" — n4 \"the way\" tied from n2 (the tree!) — r3 from n2 to n4 — the way tied to the tree?? \"place a tree ... — the way the drawing looks so far —\" — the dashes make \"the way...\" an appositive to \"a tree\" — plausible (the tree should be the way the drawing looks). r4 from n4 to c2. \"exactly where the picture needs it\" — r2 \"where\" from c1 to c3 — fine. L8.\n\n**n=103**: \"when the regions dial in tomorrow the first chart they see is Sales by month, so for their sake give the bars a gradient fill before the meeting starts — while you're at it, show both grid lines\"\n- r1 \"when\" from c3 to c1. n1 \"the regions\". c1 declarative \"dial\" modifiers in/tomorrow subject n1. n2 \"the first chart\". n3 \"they\". c2 declarative \"see\" subject n3. c3 declarative \"is\" subject n2 complement n4 (\"Sales\"). r2 \"by\" from n4 to n5 (\"month\"). k1 \"so\" conjunct c3, c4, c7. r3 \"for\" from c4 to n6 (\"their sake\"). c4 imperative \"give\" complements n7 (\"the bars\"), n8 (\"a gradient fill\"). r4 \"before\" from c4 to c5 (\"starts\" subject \"the meeting\"). r5 \"while\" from c7 to c6 (\"'re\" subject you; r6 \"at\" c6→it). c7 imperative \"show\" complement n12 (\"both grid lines\"). r7 from n2 to c2.\n- \"when the regions dial in tomorrow the first chart they see is Sales by month\" — r1 \"when\" from c3 to c1 — the when-clause attached to c3 (the \"is\" clause) — fine. k1 \"so\" joins c3, c4, c7 — the message: \"..., so for their sake give the bars a gradient fill before the meeting starts — while you're at it, show both grid lines\" — \"so\" introduces the give-command; \"while you're at it\" attaches to \"show\". The coordination k1 \"so\" joins c3 (is), c4 (give), c7 (show) — hmm, \"so\" joins the result clause with... the \"so\" clause is the give. Joining c3 and c4 with \"so\" — fine. Including c7 (show) — the \"while you're at it, show...\" — is \"show\" joined by \"so\"? The message has \"— while you're at it, show both grid lines\" — the show is an aside joined by \"while\", not \"so\". Hmm. But k1 has only \"so\" as conjunction with three conjuncts — the message has \"so\" once. Joining c7 with \"so\" — wrong conjunct? Per the settled case: \"In 'move the star so it sits under the table, then give it a thin outline', 'then' joins 'give' with 'move'.\" — analogous: here \"while... show\" should be joined by... the dash. Hmm. The settled dash case: \"— while you're at it, give it the title...\" — the \"while\" relation starts at the clause after the comma. Here r5 \"while\" from c7 to c6 — correct. But c7 also in k1 with \"so\" — is that a wrong conjunct (L3)? \"A coordination has a wrong conjunct\" — c7 joined by \"so\" — the message's \"so\" gives result of the \"is\" clause → \"give the bars...\". \"show both grid lines\" is not a result of \"so\" — it's an aside. Including it in the \"so\" coordination — questionable. Hmm. I'll accept — the next layer can sort it. Actually per \"A clause that ends a relation with a marker... a coordination that joins an imperative or interrogative clause with such a clause has a wrong conjunct: L3\" — that's about command inside relation. Here c7 is imperative joined via \"so\" coordination with c4 (imperative) — both imperatives — fine. L8.\n\n**n=104**: \"add a chart with source holdings and type bar and title Quarterly plan and labels North, South and East and values 3, 5 and 8\"\n- Similar to n=86. Let me check the dump: \n```\n@n2 source holdings; @k1 \"and\" conjunct n2, n3, n4; @n3 type bar; @n4 title; @n5 Quarterly plan; @k2 \"and\",\"and\" conjunct n5, n6, n10; @n6 labels; @n7 North; @n8 South; @n9 East; @k3 \"and\" conjunct n7,n8,n9; @n10 values; @n11 3; @n12 5; @k4 \"and\" conjunct n11,n12,n13; @n13 8; @r2 from n3 to k2; @r3 from n6 to k3; @r4 from n10 to k4.\n```\n- k1 = {source holdings, type bar, title} — the \"with\" list joined by \"and\"s — the message: \"with source holdings and type bar and title Quarterly plan and labels ... and values ...\" — k1 joins the first three items with \"and\",\"and\" — then r2 ties \"title\" to k2 = {Quarterly plan, labels, values} — hmm, \"title Quarterly plan\" — the plan is the title's content... k2 = {plan, labels, values} — the message after \"title\": \"Quarterly plan and labels North, South and East and values 3, 5 and 8\" — so k2 joins plan, labels, values with \"and\",\"and\" — matches the message's \"and\"s. r3 labels→k3 (N,S,E), r4 values→k4 (3,5,8). Structure is convoluted but matches the ands. L8. (Need to verify n=86 similarly — its k2 had conjunct $n10; in n=86, n10=\"3\"?? Let me verify with grep.)\n\n**n=105**: \"add Planning on 2026-11-18 at 10:00 UTC, 30 minutes, ID planning\"\n- c1 imperative \"add\" complement n1 (\"Planning\"). r1 \"on\" from n1 to n2 (\"2026-11-18\"). r2 \"at\" (ambiguous) from c1 to n3 (\"10:00 UTC\"). n4 \"30 minutes\". n5 \"ID planning\". k1 coordination conjunct c1, c2. r3 from n1 to n4. c2 fragment complement n5.\n- \"add Planning on 2026-11-18 at 10:00 UTC, 30 minutes, ID planning\" — the circuit: \"on 2026-11-18\" tied to \"Planning\" (r1), \"at 10:00 UTC\" from c1, \"30 minutes\" tied to Planning (r3), c2 fragment \"ID planning\" coordinated with c1 (no conjunction — the comma). Per the time settled case: \"a day, an hour or a length of time set off by commas after a phrase with a marker ('book the sync at 16:00, Friday, for 60 minutes') belongs to the clause: a relation from the clause with no marker passes, and so does a coordination with the time before it.\" Here \"2026-11-18\" is after \"on\" (a marker phrase) set off by comma — belongs to the clause — a relation from the CLAUSE passes — the circuit has r1 \"on\" from n1 (Planning) to the date — hmm, \"add Planning on 2026-11-18\" — \"on 2026-11-18\" modifies \"Planning\" or \"add\" — the settled case says a relation from the clause with no marker passes — from the nominal? \"belongs to the clause: a relation from the clause with no marker passes\" — the relation should start at the clause (c1), not at n1. Here r1 starts at n1 with marker \"on\" — \"Planning on 2026-11-18\" — hmm, that's the natural reading actually (\"Planning on [date]\"). The settled case example: \"book the sync at 16:00, Friday, for 60 minutes\" — \"Friday\" after \"at 16:00\" — belongs to the clause. Here \"2026-11-18\" directly follows \"Planning\" with \"on\" — it's \"Planning on 2026-11-18\" — a marker phrase attached to Planning — fine. \"30 minutes\" set off by commas after the marker phrase \"at 10:00 UTC\" — belongs to the clause — the circuit ties it to n1 (Planning) via r3 (no marker) — the settled case says a relation from the clause passes; from the nominal \"Planning\"? Hmm — \"add Planning ..., 30 minutes\" — 30 minutes is the duration of the planning — tied to Planning — plausible. The failure in the settled case was \"a coordination with what the verb acts on\" — here it's a relation, not a coordination with the acted-on thing. Eh — r3 from n1 to n4 — a relation from \"Planning\" — I'll accept. \"ID planning\" as fragment coordinated — fine. L8.\n\n**n=106**: \"hey — I always want the arrows dashed, so set the existing ones now\"\n- c1 declarative discourse \"hey\" modifier \"always\" verb \"want\" subject n1 (\"I\"). n2 \"the arrows\". c2 nonfinite \"dashed\" subject n2. k1 \"so\" conjunct c1, c3. c3 imperative \"set\" modifier \"now\" complement n3 (\"the existing ones\"). r1 from c1 to c2.\n- \"the arrows dashed\" — c2 nonfinite \"dashed\" with subject \"the arrows\", tied to c1 via r1 — \"I want the arrows dashed\" — \"dashed\" as participle complement — fine (settled: participle tied to the thing). L8.\n\n**n=107**: \"Let's do a bar chart of monthly average claims across 2024, by region, restricted to policies that stayed active all year, the top six regions, ties kept, sorted from high to low, with the y axis starting at zero, make the bars blue\"\n- c1 imperative verb \"Let\" complement n1 (\"'s\"). c2 nonfinite \"do\" complement n2 (\"a bar chart\"). r1 \"of\" from n2 to n3 (\"monthly average claims\"). r2 \"across\" from n3 to n4 (\"2024\"). r3 \"by\" from n3 to n5 (\"region\"). c3 nonfinite \"restricted\". r4 \"to\" from c3 to n6 (\"policies\"). r5 \"that\" from n6 to c4 (\"stayed\" modifier active complement \"all year\"). n8 \"the top six regions\". c5 nonfinite \"kept\" subject n9 (\"ties\"). c6 nonfinite \"sorted\". r6 \"from\" from c6 to n10 (\"high\"). r7 \"to\" from c6 to n11 (\"low\"). r8 \"with\" from n8 to c7 (\"starting\" subject \"the y axis\"; r9 \"at\" c7→\"zero\"). c8 declarative \"make\" modifier \"blue\" subject n8 complement n14 (\"the bars\"). r10 c1→c2, r11 c1→c8, r12 n2→c3, r13 n8→c5, r14 n8→c6.\n- \"Let's do a bar chart...\" — \"Let\" imperative verb, \"'s\" complement — per settled let's case, passes. \"make the bars blue\" — c8 declarative with subject n8 (\"the top six regions\")!! \"make the bars blue\" — a command at the end — the circuit made it a declarative clause with subject \"the top six regions\" — WRONG: \"the top six regions make the bars blue\"?? The message: \"..., with the y axis starting at zero, make the bars blue\" — \"make\" is a command (imperative). The circuit: c8 declarative verb \"make\" subject=$n8 (\"the top six regions\") complement=$n14 (\"the bars\") modifier \"blue\". That states \"the top six regions make the bars blue\" — a wrong statement! L1: form wrong (command written as declarative) and subject wrong (\"a subject the words do not show\" — L4). First: L1 (form: command written as declarative). Per the settled case: \"a verb after 'and' or 'then' that follows a clause with a subject and an auxiliary...\" — here \"make\" follows a comma after \"with the y axis starting at zero\" — no \"and\"/\"then\" — so it's a fresh command → imperative; written declarative with a borrowed subject → L1. Note: \"'make the bars blue' is a command but is written as a declarative clause with 'the top six regions' as subject\". L1.\n\n**n=108**: \"I want the Draft box split into two boxes side by side, with the incoming arrow on the left one and the outgoing arrow on the right\"\n- n1 \"I\". c1 declarative \"want\" subject n1. n2 \"the Draft box\". c2 nonfinite \"split\" subject n2. r1 \"into\" from c2 to n3 (\"two boxes\"). n4 \"side\". r2 \"by\" from n4 to n5 (\"side\"). r3 \"with\" from n3 to k1. n6 \"the incoming arrow\". r4 \"on\" from n6 to n7 (\"the left one\"). k1 \"and\" conjunct n6, n8. n8 \"the outgoing arrow\". r5 \"on\" from n8 to n9 (\"the right\"). r6 from c1 to c2. r7 from n3 to n4.\n- \"side by side\" — r2 \"by\" from \"side\" to \"side\" — fixed phrase \"side by side\" may be held whole as a modifier — here decomposed: n4 \"side\" tied from n3 (r7), r2 \"by\" side→side — \"two boxes side by side\" — plausible decomposition. \"with the incoming arrow on the left one and the outgoing arrow on the right\" — r3 \"with\" from n3 (two boxes) to k1 — the with-phrase attached to \"two boxes\" — plausible. L8.\n\n**n=109**: \"create a doughnut chart called Sales 7 for Jan and Feb with datasets [{\"label\": \"North\", \"data\": [7, 9]}, {\"label\": \"South\", \"data\": [8, 10]}]\"\n- c1 imperative \"create\" complement n1 (\"a doughnut chart\"). c2 nonfinite \"called\" complement n2 (\"Sales\"). n3 \"7\". r1 \"for\" from n2 to k1. n4 \"Jan\". k1 \"and\" conjunct n4, n5. n5 \"Feb\". r2 \"with\" from n1 to n6 (\"datasets\"). n7 \"label North data\" (dependents label/North, head data). n8 \"7\", n9 \"9\". n10 \"label\", n11 \"South\", n12 \"data\". n13 \"8\", n14 \"10\". r3 n1→c2, r4 n2→n3, r5 n6→n7, r6 n7→n8, r7 n7→n10, r8 n8→n9, r9 n10→n11, r10 n10→n12, r11 n12→n13, r12 n13→n14.\n- The JSON datasets parsed into a chain of relations — messy but every word tied. \"called Sales 7\" — r4 from \"Sales\" to \"7\" — fine. L8.\n\n**n=110**: \"while you're there, I want the marker interval 3 on the revenue trend and I'd like the series name Trajectory on the revenue trend\"\n- r1 \"while\" from c2 to c1. n1 \"you\". c1 declarative \"'re\" modifier \"there\" subject n1. n2 \"I\". c2 declarative \"want\" subject n2 complement n3 (\"the marker interval\"). n4 \"3\". r2 \"on\" from n3 to n5 (\"the revenue trend\"). k1 \"and\" conjunct c2, c3. n6 \"I\". c3 declarative auxiliary \"'d\" verb \"like\" subject n6 complement n7 (\"the series name\"). n8 \"Trajectory\". r3 \"on\" from c3 to n9 (\"the revenue trend\"). r4 from n3 to n4. r5 from n7 to n8.\n- \"the marker interval 3\" — \"3\" tied to \"the marker interval\" (r4) — number after noun — passes. \"while you're there\" — r1 \"while\" from c2 to c1 — the while-clause before the main clause — relation from main to it — fine. L8.\n\n**n=111**: \"change the backdrop to a garden full of roses\"\n- c1 imperative \"change\" complement n1 (\"the backdrop\"). r1 \"to\" from c1 to n2 (\"a garden\" dependent \"full\"). r2 \"of\" from n2 to n3 (\"roses\").\n- \"full of roses\" — \"full\" as dependent of \"garden\" — \"a garden full of roses\" — participle-adjective as dependent — fine. L8.\n\n**n=112**: \"half the stand-up was grumbling that the numbers were impossible to read from the corridor screen; to settle it, I'd like the numbers back on the right on the sales chart — while you're at it, how about top numbers in tens? going forward, also make the font a bit bigger\"\n- n1 \"half the stand-up\". c1 declarative auxiliary \"was\" verb \"grumbling\" subject n1. r1 \"that\" from c1 to c2. c2 declarative \"were\" modifier \"impossible\" subject n2 (\"the numbers\"). r2 \"to\" from c2 to c3 (nonfinite \"read\"). r3 \"from\" from c3 to n3 (\"the corridor screen\"). r4 \"to\" from c5 to c4 (nonfinite \"settle\" complement \"it\"). n5 \"I\". c5 interrogative auxiliary \"'d\" verb \"like\" modifier \"back\" subject n5 complement n6 (\"the numbers\"). Hmm — \"I'd like the numbers back on the right...\" — declarative! Written as interrogative → L1. Same as n=67. Note: \"'I'd like the numbers back' is a statement but is written as an interrogative clause\". L1.\n- (Rest: r5 \"on\" from c5 to n7 (\"the right\"), r6 \"on\" from n7 to n8 (\"the sales chart\"), r7 \"while\" from c7 to c6, c6 \"'re\" you, r8 \"at\" c6→it, c7 fragment modifier \"how\" ellipsis c5, r9 \"about\" from c7 to n11 (\"top numbers\"), r10 \"in\" from n11 to n12 (\"tens\"), c8 nonfinite \"going\" modifier \"forward\", c9 imperative modifier \"also\" verb \"make\" modifier \"bigger\" complements n13 (\"the font\"), n14 (\"a bit\"), k1 conjunct c5, c7, r11 from n9 to c8.) The main error: c5 interrogative for a statement. L1.\n\n**n=113**: \"No, make the avatar shaven after all, because the beard still shows.\"\n- c1 imperative discourse \"No\" verb \"make\" modifier \"shaven\" modifiers \"after\",\"all\" complement n1 (\"the avatar\"). r1 \"because\" from c1 to c2 (\"shows\" subject \"the beard\" modifier \"still\").\n- \"No,\" at start as discourse — fine. \"after all\" as modifiers — fixed phrase — fine. L8.\n\n**n=114**: \"mirror the boat top to bottom but not left to right, so the bow still points at the harbour\"\n- c1 imperative \"mirror\" complements n1 (\"the boat\"), n2 (\"top\"). r1 \"to\" from n2 to n3 (\"bottom\"). k1 \"but\" conjunct c1, c2. c2 fragment modifier \"not\" modifier \"left\" ellipsis c1. r2 \"to\" from c2 to n4 (\"right\"). r3 \"so\" from c1 to c3 (\"points\" subject \"the bow\" modifier still). r4 \"at\" from c3 to n6 (\"the harbour\").\n- \"but not left to right\" — c2 fragment with ellipsis c1 (\"mirror\"), modifiers \"not\",\"left\", r2 \"to\" from c2 to \"right\" — \"not\" as modifier of the fragment — hmm, \"not\" is a negation word — held as modifier, not negation! L1: \"An auxiliary or negation word is held in another place, or negates the wrong clause.\" Here c2 has modifier=\"not\" instead of negation=\"not\". The negation of \"left to right\" is lost (the fragment would read \"mirror left to right\" positively). L1. Note: \"'not' in 'but not left to right' is a negation word but is held as a modifier of the fragment, so the negation is lost\". L1.\n\n**n=115**: \"for the monthly ops review on Friday I need the revenue trend legible from the back of the conference room, so make the reference line dashed, grey and horizontal before we circulate it wider and then make the font a bit bigger\"\n- r1 \"for\" from c1 to n1 (\"the monthly ops review\"). r2 \"on\" from n1 to n2 (\"Friday\"). n3 \"I\". c1 declarative \"need\" modifier \"legible\" subject n3 complement n4 (\"the revenue trend\"). Good — declarative this time. r3 \"from\" from c1 to n5 (\"the back\"). r4 \"of\" from n5 to n6 (\"the conference room\"). c2 imperative \"make\" modifiers \"dashed\",\"grey\" discourse \"and\" modifier \"horizontal\" complement n7 (\"the reference line\"). k1 \"so\",\"and\" conjunct c1, c2, c4. r5 \"before\" from c2 to c3 (\"circulate\" subject \"we\" modifier \"wider\" complement \"it\"). c4 imperative modifier \"then\" verb \"make\" modifier \"bigger\" complements n10 (\"the font\"), n11 (\"a bit\").\n- \"dashed, grey and horizontal\" — \"and\" between modifier words of one clause → discourse — settled, passes. k1 \"so\",\"and\" — the message: \"..., so make the reference line ... before we circulate it wider and then make the font a bit bigger\" — \"so\" before \"make the reference line\", \"and\" in \"dashed, grey and horizontal\" (used as discourse of c2), \"then\" before the second \"make\". k1 conjunctions \"so\" and \"and\" — hmm, the message has \"so\" once, \"and\" once (in the grey list), \"then\" once. k1 uses \"so\" and \"and\" as conjunctions joining c1, c2, c4 — the \"and\" used both as discourse of c2 AND as conjunction of k1? That would double-count \"and\"... unless the checker... whatever. The \"and\" in k1 — the message's \"and\" is between \"grey\" and \"horizontal\" (modifiers of c2) — using it as the coordination conjunction joining c2 and c4 — hmm, but c4 is joined by \"then\" (\"and then make the font...\"). Messy. The \"then\" is modifier of c4. Eh — plausible enough? The coordination k1 joins the need-clause, the make-reference-line, and the make-font clauses. \"so\" joins... I'll accept L8. Hmm — but wait, similar to n=67 where I gave L1 for the interrogative — here c1 is declarative \"need\" — correct. L8.\n\n**n=116**: \"one pie chart, the wedges rides 30%, repairs 25%, spares 45%\"\n- n1 \"one pie chart\". n2 \"the wedges\". c1 declarative \"rides\" subject n2 complement n3 (\"30 %\"). c2 declarative \"repairs\" complement n4 (\"25 %\"). c3 declarative \"spares\" complement n5 (\"45 %\"). c4 fragment complement n1. k1 coordination c4, c1, c2, c3.\n- \"the wedges rides 30%, repairs 25%, spares 45%\" — \"rides\" is a misspelling of \"rides\"?? Probably \"rides\"→\"rides\"... The intended: \"the wedges: rides 30%, repairs 25%, spares 45%\" — labels. \"rides\", \"repairs\", \"spares\" are NOUNS (labels) made verbs of declarative clauses → L1 per settled (\"A noun made its verb is L1\"). Note: \"'rides', 'repairs' and 'spares' are nouns naming the wedges, but each is written as the verb of a declarative clause\". L1.\n\n**n=117**: \"Could you what values does Lunch orders show?. Thanks!\"\n- c1 interrogative verb \"Could\" subject n1 (\"you\"). n2 \"what values\". c2 interrogative auxiliary \"does\" verb \"show\" subject n3 (\"Lunch orders\") complement n2. c3 fragment discourse \"Thanks\". k1 coordination c1, c2.\n- Garbled \"Could you what values does Lunch orders show?\" — the circuit: c1 \"Could\" interrogative verb with subject \"you\" — hmm, \"Could you\" — per settled: \"'Could you move it?' is one interrogative clause: 'Could' is an auxiliary, 'you' the subject.\" Here \"Could\" is written as the VERB of c1, not an auxiliary — with no main verb in c1! L1: \"an auxiliary while the main verb stands elsewhere\" — \"Could\" as verb while... hmm. The message: \"Could you what values does Lunch orders show?\" — garbled. The circuit has c1 (Could, subject you) coordinated with c2 (does show, subject Lunch orders, complement what values). Per the settled case, \"Could you ...\" should be one interrogative clause with \"Could\" as auxiliary. Here \"Could\" is the verb of c1 and \"show\" the verb of c2 — two clauses. Is that wrong? \"Could you what values does X show\" — the intended: \"Could you [tell me] what values does Lunch orders show?\" — \"Could you\" + the question clause. The circuit's split: c1 \"Could you\" (interrogative, verb Could) + c2 \"what values does Lunch orders show\" — actually that's a reasonable decomposition of the garbled message! \"Could you\" as an elliptical tag clause — settled: \"An elliptical tag ('would you', 'if you can') may be a clause of its own with the auxiliary as verb.\" — \"could you\" as a tag clause with \"could\" as verb — passes by that settled case! Hmm — \"Could you what values does Lunch orders show?\" — \"Could you [show]?\" + \"what values does Lunch orders show?\" — the circuit's reading: c1 = \"could you\" tag, c2 = the question — k1 joins them. Per the elliptical tag case, this passes. L8. Hmm — but is c2 tied? k1 joins c1, c2 — tied. \"Thanks!\" fragment discourse — fine. L8.\n\n**n=118**: \"please to insert a picture of the old culvert and move it to the right corner\"\n- c1 fragment discourse \"please\". r1 \"to\" from c1 to k1. c2 nonfinite \"insert\" complement n1 (\"a picture\"). r2 \"of\" from n1 to n2 (\"the old culvert\"). k1 \"and\" conjunct c2, c3. c3 nonfinite \"move\" complement n3 (\"it\"). r3 \"to\" from c3 to n4 (\"the right corner\").\n- \"please to insert a picture ... and move it ...\" — \"to insert... and move...\" — the infinitive \"to\" governs both \"insert\" and \"move\" — k1 \"and\" joins c2, c3 — r1 \"to\" from c1 to k1 — the \"to\" relation ends at the coordination — settled: \"The 'to' of an infinitive is the marker of the relation that ends at the nonfinite clause. When nothing stands before it ('to fix the details, edit the members'), the relation starts at the main clause.\" Here \"please to insert...\" — \"to\" relation from c1 (fragment \"please\") to the coordination of insert/move — fine. L8.\n\n**n=119**: \"set the bend of arrow from Start to Review to 26 and the x to 120 and the y to 80\"\n- c1 imperative \"set\" complement n1 (\"the bend\"). r1 \"of\" from n1 to n2 (\"arrow\"). r2 \"from\" from n1 to n3 (\"Start\"). r3 \"to\" from n3 to n4 (\"Review\"). r4 \"to\" (ambiguous) from c1 to n5 (\"26\"). k1 \"and\",\"and\" conjunct c1, c2. n6 \"the x\". r5 \"to\" from n6 to n7 (\"120\"). n8 \"the y\". r6 \"to\" from n8 to n9 (\"80\"). c2 fragment complement k2 ellipsis c1. k2 coordination conjunct n6, n8.\n- \"set the bend of arrow from Start to Review to 26\" — the \"to 26\" — r4 from c1 — fine. \"and the x to 120 and the y to 80\" — c2 fragment with ellipsis c1 (\"set\"), complement k2 = {the x, the y}, each with \"to\" relations to 120/80. k1 \"and\",\"and\" joins c1, c2 — two \"and\"s — the message has two \"and\"s (\"to 26 and the x\", \"120 and the y\"). k1 uses both — but the second \"and\" is between \"the x to 120\" and \"the y to 80\" — which are conjuncts of k2, not of k1! Hmm — k2 has no conjunction listed. The message's second \"and\" joins \"the x to 120\" and \"the y to 80\" — should be k2's conjunction. k1 uses \"and\",\"and\" — stealing both. Eh — the coordination structure: c1 (set bend...) and c2 (set x, y) joined by the first \"and\"; k2 joins x and y — with the second \"and\" consumed by k1... The words still all stand somewhere. Wrong conjunct? k1's conjuncts c1, c2 — fine. k2's conjuncts n6, n8 — fine. The conjunction words placement is off but... \"A coordination has a wrong conjunct\" — conjuncts look right. Accept L8.\n\n**n=120**: \"add a cat 260 Take 2\"\n- c1 imperative \"add\" complement n1 (\"a cat\"). n2 \"260\". c2 imperative verb \"Take\" complements n2, n3 (\"2\"). k1 coordination c1, c2.\n- \"add a cat 260 Take 2\" — garbled. \"Take\" as verb of imperative c2 with complement \"260\" and \"2\" — \"Take 2\" — hmm, \"260 Take 2\" — like a scene name \"cat 260 Take 2\"? The circuit reads \"add a cat 260\" + \"Take 2\" as a command. Plausible-ish? \"Take\" can be a verb. The message has no other clear verb... \"add\" is c1's verb. \"Take 2\" as imperative — plausible (like \"Take 2\" = scene label). \"260\" as complement of \"Take\" — hmm, \"260\" tied to \"Take\" — but \"260\" follows \"cat\" — per the number-after-noun case, \"260\" could be a dependent of \"cat\" or own nominal tied to \"cat\" — here it's tied as complement of \"Take\" — the message \"a cat 260\" — \"260\" belongs with \"cat\"! The circuit: n2 \"260\" is complement of c2 (Take), not tied to \"a cat\". Is \"260\" tied to anything in its sentence? It's complement of c2; c2 is a conjunct of k1 with c1. Hmm — \"a cat 260\" pairing not written: L6? \"A pairing the words give is not written: 'x 120' with '120' in another wire and tied to nothing\" — here \"260\" is tied (to Take's clause), but the pairing \"cat 260\" is not written — the words give \"a cat 260\" (a cat named/numbered 260). The circuit separates: add a cat; Take 260 2. Lost: the cat is \"260 Take 2\"? Eh — garbled message; the circuit's reading \"add a cat; 260; Take 2\" — hmm. I'll accept L8 (plausible; \"Take 2\" as a label command).\n\n**n=121**: \"draw the diagram, then check the profit area is above zero\"\n- c1 imperative \"draw\" complement n1 (\"the diagram\"). k1 \"then\" conjunct c1, c2. c2 imperative \"check\". n2 \"the profit area\". c3 declarative \"is\" subject n2. r1 \"above\" from c3 to n3 (\"zero\"). r2 from c2 to c3.\n- \"check the profit area is above zero\" — c2 imperative \"check\" with no complement; c3 \"is\" clause tied via r2 from c2; \"the profit area\" subject of c3. Settled: \"A clause that opens with a question word after a verb of telling or asking ('tell me which events are scheduled') must be tied to that verb's clause; any relation is enough.\" — \"check [that] the profit area is above zero\" — c3 tied to c2 via r2 — passes. L8.\n\n**n=122**: \"would you make that one look nicer\"\n- c1 interrogative auxiliary \"would\" verb \"make\" subject n1 (\"you\"). n2 \"that one\". c2 nonfinite \"look\" modifier \"nicer\" subject n2. r1 from c1 to c2.\n- \"make that one look nicer\" — c2 nonfinite \"look\" subject \"that one\" tied to c1 — \"make X look nicer\" — causative — fine. L8.\n\n**n=123**: \"slide block 15 under block 2, then slide block 15 left to block 14\"\n- c1 imperative \"slide\" complement n1 (\"block\"). n2 \"15\". r1 \"under\" from n1 to n3 (\"block\"). n4 \"2\". k1 \"then\" conjunct c1, c3. n5 \"slide block\" (dependent slide, head block). n6 \"15\". c2 nonfinite verb \"left\"?? — c2: form=nonfinite verb=\"left\" — \"left\" as a VERB! \"slide block 15 left to block 14\" — \"left\" is a direction (modifier), not a verb. The circuit: c2 nonfinite verb=\"left\", r2 \"to\" from n5 to n7 (\"block\"), n8 \"14\", c3 fragment complement n5 ellipsis c1, r3 n1→n2, r4 n3→n4, r5 n5→c2, r6 n5→n6, r7 n7→n8.\n- \"then slide block 15 left to block 14\" — the circuit made \"slide\" a dependent of \"block\" (n5 = \"slide block\") and \"left\" the verb of a nonfinite clause c2, tied from n5 (r5). So \"slide block 15 left to block 14\" → fragment c3 (complement \"slide block 15\", ellipsis c1) with \"left\" as a nonfinite verb clause... \"left\" is not a verb here — it's a direction modifier. L1: \"Its verb is a word that is no verb here.\" \"left\" can be a verb (to leave) — \"block left\" — grammar allows \"left\" as verb?? \"the block left\" — past tense of leave — \"slide block 15 left\" — hmm, \"left\" as verb would mean \"departed\" — \"slide block 15 left to block 14\" — reading \"block 15 left to block 14\" — eh. Grammar allows \"left\" as a verb (past of leave) — \"A word that grammar allows as a noun or as a verb... passes\" — that's noun-or-verb; \"left\" is primarily a verb form (leave). Hmm! But in context \"slide ... left\" — \"left\" is clearly a direction. The words: \"slide block 15 left to block 14\" — after the verb \"slide\" (ellipsis), \"block 15\" object, \"left\" — direction modifier, \"to block 14\" — relation. Writing \"left\" as the verb of a nonfinite clause states \"block 15 left...\" — wrong. L1? Or is it acceptable because \"left\" can be a verb? The settled case: \"A word that grammar allows as a noun or as a verb: 'make y axis scale linear'\" — passes. \"left\" — grammar allows as verb (leave, past). Hmm. But \"slide block 15 left\" — with \"slide\" as the command, \"left\" as another verb is weird. I think L1: \"'left' is a direction here, not a verb, but it is written as the verb of a nonfinite clause\". Compare n=12/n=149 where \"left\" appears as modifier of a slide clause (\"then est block 1 left until...\") — there \"left\" was a modifier. Here it's the verb. I'll grade L1.\n- Hmm, wait — alternatively the message might be \"slide block 15 left\" where the whole second command is \"slide block 15 left to block 14\" — the circuit's c3 fragment with ellipsis c1 covers \"slide block 15\" and then \"left to block 14\" as c2... The error stands: \"left\" as verb. L1.\n\n**n=124**: \"create a scatter chart called Sales 4 for Jan and Feb with datasets [...]\"\n- Same as n=109 pattern. c1 imperative \"create\" complement n1. c2 nonfinite \"called\" complement n2 (\"Sales\"). n3 \"4\". r1 \"for\" from n2 to k1. n4 \"Jan\", k1 \"and\" conjunct n4, n5, n5 \"Feb\". r2 \"with\" from n1 to n6 (\"datasets\"). n7 \"label North data\"... r3 n1→c2, r4 n2→n3, r5 n6→n7, etc. L8 (same as 109).\n\n**n=125**: \"the olive treatment for the shape, and olive lettered inside\"\n- n1 \"the olive treatment\". r1 \"for\" from n1 to n2 (\"the shape\"). k1 \"and\" conjunct n1, n3. n3 \"olive lettered\" (dependent olive, head lettered). c1 fragment modifier \"inside\" complement k1.\n- \"olive lettered inside\" — \"lettered\" as head of nominal — \"olive lettered\" — participle \"lettered\" as head — hmm, \"a participle that names or describes... is the verb of a nonfinite clause tied to the thing. Written as the marker of a relation between the thing and its name, it also passes.\" Here \"lettered\" as head of a nominal — not covered — but \"olive lettered\" as a unit (the lettering olive) — plausible? \"and olive lettered inside\" — \"and [the] olive [is] lettered inside\" — eh. The circuit: n3 nominal \"olive lettered\" (head lettered) in k1 with n1, all in fragment c1 with modifier \"inside\". Plausible continuation. L8.\n\n**n=126**: \"I walked the team through Revenue trend this morning; nobody argued with the data, only with how it reads at a glance, so as a first pass how about line thinner? and then let's have the marker borders thicker on the revenue trend, then we revisit next week, also put the y axis on the right\"\n- n1 \"I\". c1 declarative \"walked\" subject n1 complements n2 (\"the team\"), n4 (\"this morning\"). r1 \"through\" from c1 to n3 (\"Revenue trend\"). n5 \"nobody\". r2 \"with\" from c2 to n6 (\"the data\"). r3 \"with\" from c2 to c3. c3 declarative modifier \"how\" verb \"reads\" subject n7 (\"it\"). c2 interrogative verb \"argued\" modifier \"only\" modifier \"how\" subject n5. Hmm — c2: \"nobody argued with the data, only with how it reads at a glance\" — declarative! Written as interrogative → L1. Also \"only\" as modifier — fine-ish. Note: \"'nobody argued with the data' is a statement but is written as an interrogative clause\". L1.\n- (Rest: r4 \"at\" from c3 to n8 (\"a glance\"), k1 \"so\",\"and\" conjunct c1, c2, c4, c7, c8, r5 \"as\" from c2 to n9 (\"a first pass\"), r6 \"about\" from c2 to n10 (\"line thinner\" — dependent thinner!), c4 imperative modifier \"then\" verb \"let\" complement n11 (\"'s\"), c5 nonfinite \"have\", n12 \"the marker\", c6 declarative \"borders\" modifier \"thicker\" subject n12 — \"borders\" as VERB of declarative c6! \"the marker borders thicker\" — \"borders\" is a noun here (the marker borders) — made verb → L1 (noun made verb). r7 \"on\" from c6 to n13, c7 declarative modifier \"then\" verb \"revisit\" subject \"we\" complement n15 (\"next week\"), c8 imperative modifier \"also\" verb \"put\" complement n16 (\"the y axis\"), r8 \"on\" from c8 to n17 (\"the right\"), r9 c4→c5, r10 c5→c6.) Multiple L1 issues: c2 interrogative, c6 \"borders\" as verb. First L1. Note can mention both or one. I'll note the \"argued\" interrogative and \"borders\" noun-as-verb. One sentence: \"'nobody argued' is a statement written as an interrogative, and 'borders' in 'the marker borders thicker' is a noun written as the verb of a declarative clause\". L1.\n\n**n=127**: \"and also Sales by month: grid line style dash-dot\"\n- c1 fragment discourse \"and\" complement n1 (\"also Sales\" — dependent also, head Sales). r1 \"by\" from n1 to n2 (\"month\"). n3 \"grid line style dash-dot\" (dependents grid/line/style, head dash-dot). r2 from n1 to n3.\n- \"and also Sales by month: grid line style dash-dot\" — \"and\" at start as discourse — fine. \"also\" as dependent of \"Sales\" — hmm, \"also\" is a modifier-ish word — held as dependent of the nominal — eh. \"Sales by month\" with \"also\" dependent — \"also Sales by month\" — plausible. L8.\n\n**n=128**: \"This year and last side by side: order value by delivery time\"\n- n1 \"This year\". k1 \"and\" conjunct n1, n2. n2 \"last\". n3 \"side\". r1 \"by\" from n3 to n4 (\"side\"). n5 \"order value\". r2 \"by\" from n5 to n6 (\"delivery time\"). c1 fragment complement k1, n3, n5.\n- \"This year and last side by side: order value by delivery time\" — \"side by side\" — n3 \"side\" with r1 \"by\" to \"side\" — fine. c1 fragment with complements: k1 (This year and last), \"side\", \"order value\" — plausible. L8.\n\n**n=129**: \"when the board dials in tomorrow morning the first chart they open is Revenue trend, so for their sake make it into a bar chart before the meeting starts. and then make the font a bit bigger\"\n- r1 \"when\" from c3 to c1. n1 \"the board\". c1 declarative \"dials\" modifier \"in\" subject n1 complement n2 (\"tomorrow morning\"). n3 \"the first chart\". n4 \"they\". c2 declarative \"open\" subject n4. c3 declarative \"is\" subject n3 complement n5 (\"Revenue trend\"). k1 \"so\" conjunct c3, c4. r2 \"for\" from c4 to n6 (\"their sake\"). c4 imperative \"make\" complement n7 (\"it\"). r3 \"into\" from c4 to n8 (\"a bar chart\"). r4 \"before\" from c4 to c5 (\"starts\" subject \"the meeting\"). c6 imperative discourse \"and\" modifier \"then\" verb \"make\" modifier \"bigger\" complements n10 (\"the font\"), n11 (\"a bit\"). r5 from n3 to c2.\n- \"and then make the font a bit bigger\" — new sentence starting with \"and\" — held as discourse of c6 — settled: \"may be a discourse word, as at the start of a message, or the conjunction of a coordination of the clauses of the two sentences\" — passes. c6 imperative — fine. L8.\n\n**n=130**: \"Revenue by month of the order date, top five regions only\"\n- n1 \"Revenue\". r1 \"by\" from n1 to n2 (\"month\"). r2 \"of\" from n2 to n3 (\"the order date\"). c1 imperative verb \"top\" modifier \"only\" complement n4 (\"five regions\"). c2 fragment complement n1. r3 from n1 to c1.\n- \"top five regions only\" — \"top\" as the VERB of an imperative clause! \"Revenue by month of the order date, top five regions only\" — \"top\" here is... \"top five regions\" = the top five regions — \"top\" is an adjective/quantifier, not a command. The circuit made c1 imperative with verb \"top\" and complement \"five regions\" — states a command \"top five regions!\" — the message doesn't command \"top\" — \"top\" is part of \"top five regions only\" (a restriction). L1: \"'top' is an adjective in 'top five regions only', not a verb, but it is written as the verb of an imperative clause\". Hmm — \"top\" can be a verb (to top) — grammar allows noun/verb ambiguity... \"top five regions\" — \"top\" as verb would need \"top the five regions\" — with \"the\" — here no \"the\": \"top five regions only\" — as a command \"top five regions!\" — hmm, actually \"top\" as imperative verb with \"five regions\" as complement — \"top five regions!\" = \"exceed five regions!\" — semantically odd but grammatical? The settled case: \"A word that grammar allows as a noun or as a verb: 'make y axis scale linear'\" — passes. \"top\" is allowed as a verb. Hmm. But context: \"Revenue by month of the order date, top five regions only\" — clearly \"top five regions only\" is a noun phrase (restriction to top five). Writing it as a command \"top five regions\" is a wrong statement? The next layer would read a command to top... I think L1: the part \"top five regions only\" has no verb — it's a fragment (like \"pie radius smaller\"); \"top\" made the verb → per settled \"A noun made its verb is L1\" — \"top\" here is an adjective... The settled fragment case: \"A message or a part with no verb (...) is a fragment... A noun made its verb is L1.\" \"top\" in \"top five regions\" is an adjective modifying regions (or a noun?). Either way, making it the verb of a command is wrong — the message doesn't say \"top!\". L1. Note: \"'top five regions only' is a part with no verb, but 'top' is written as the verb of an imperative clause\". L1.\n\n**n=131**: \"add a star called dog; then put dog below owner.\"\n- c1 imperative \"add\" complement n1 (\"a star\"). n2 \"called dog\" (dependent called, head dog). k1 \"then\" conjunct c1, c2. c2 imperative \"put\" complement n3 (\"dog\"). r1 \"below\" from c2 to n4 (\"owner\"). r2 from n1 to n2.\n- \"called dog\" — \"called\" as dependent of \"dog\" — participle naming — settled: \"A participle that names or describes ('a group called g2'...) is the verb of a nonfinite clause tied to the thing. Written as the marker of a relation between the thing and its name, it also passes.\" Here \"called\" as dependent of the nominal \"dog\" — not one of the two passing options! Hmm — \"a star called dog\" — options: (a) nonfinite clause \"called\" tied to the star, with \"dog\" as its complement; (b) \"called\" as the marker of a relation between \"star\" and \"dog\". The circuit: n2 = nominal with head \"dog\" and dependent \"called\", tied from n1 (r2, no marker) — so \"called\" is a dependent inside the dog nominal — neither (a) nor (b). Is that L3? \"A linking word is held as a modifier or a dependent\" — \"called\" is a participle, not a linking word per se... Hmm. But it's held as a dependent of \"dog\" — the word \"called\" becomes part of the name \"called dog\" — the message says the star is CALLED dog — the naming relation lost? The star is tied to the \"called dog\" nominal — the next layer sees \"a star\" — \"called dog\" — eh, it can continue (the name is dog). I'll accept L8. Hmm, or L3... The settled case gives two passing forms; this is a third form. \"Where several readings pass\" — the listed ones pass; others may fail. But the general principle: reject only wrong statement or lost info. Holding \"called\" as dependent of \"dog\" — no wrong statement, info (name is dog) preserved. L8.\n\n**n=132**: \"I showed Budget split round the team; they didn't argue with the numbers but not with how it reads at a glance, so as a first pass make the legend smaller, then we revisit next week; plus show the percentages\"\n- n1 \"I\". c1 declarative \"showed\" subject n1 complement n2 (\"Budget split\"). r1 \"round\" from n2 to n3 (\"the team\"). n4 \"they\". c2 declarative auxiliary \"did\" negation \"n't\" verb \"argue\" subject n4. r2 \"with\" from c2 to n5 (\"the numbers\"). k1 \"but\" conjunct r2, r3. r3 negation \"not\" marker \"with\" from c2 to c3. c3 declarative modifier \"how\" verb \"reads\" subject n6 (\"it\"). r4 \"at\" from c3 to n7 (\"a glance\"). r5 \"so\" from c5 to c4. r6 \"as\" from c4 to n8 (\"a first pass\"). c4 imperative \"make\" modifier \"smaller\" complement n9 (\"the legend\"). c5 declarative modifier \"then\" verb \"revisit\" subject n10 (\"we\") complement n11 (\"next week\"). k2 \"plus\" conjunct c1, c2, c5, c6. c6 imperative \"show\" complement n12 (\"the percentages\").\n- Issues: (a) k1 \"but\" joins r2 (with the numbers) and r3 (not with how...) — coordinating the two \"with\" relations — \"argued with the numbers but not with how...\" — plausible! (b) k2 \"plus\" joins c1, c2, c5, c6 — the message: \"I showed...; they didn't argue..., so as a first pass make the legend smaller, then we revisit next week; plus show the percentages\" — \"plus\" joins \"show the percentages\" with what precedes — including c1, c2, c5? The \"plus\" is at \"; plus show...\" — the wire before is c5 (revisit). Joining c1 and c2 too — across semicolons — per the settled case (\"and cannot join 'give' with 'I walked' across 'make'\") — here \"plus\" joining \"show\" with \"I showed\" and \"they didn't argue\" across \"make the legend smaller\" (c4) — c4 is NOT a conjunct of k2 but sits between c2/c5 and c6 — \"A coordination with a clause between two of its conjuncts that belongs to none of them fails: L3\" — c4 (make the legend smaller) belongs to... r5 \"so\" from c5 to c4 — c4 is inside the \"so\" relation of c5. Does c4 \"belong to\" c5? Hmm. In the rules' example, \"make\" was the clause between \"I walked\" and \"give\" and belonged to none. Here c4 belongs to c5 via \"so\"... ambiguous. (c) c5 \"then we revisit next week\" — declarative with modifier \"then\" — \"then\" as modifier of c5 — \"then\" is a word of order — as a clause modifier — fine (\"Now at the start... may be a discourse word or a modifier of its clause\" — \"then\" similar). OK.\n- Hmm. The k2 coordination with 4 conjuncts including c1, c2 from earlier sentences — the settled case example says \"and\" cannot join across a middle clause. Here \"plus\" joins \"show\" with \"I showed\" across \"make\" (c4) and \"revisit\" (c5 is a conjunct)... c4 sits between c2 and c6 in the message and is not a conjunct — but it belongs to c5 (a conjunct) via \"so\". I think this is borderline. Let me instead check for a clearer error... \"Budget split\" — n2 head \"split\" dependent \"Budget\" — \"I showed Budget split round the team\" — \"split\" as head of nominal — \"Budget split\" — a name — fine. r1 \"round\" from n2 to \"the team\" — \"round the team\" — fine.\n- I'll accept L8 for n=132 — the coordination is broad but not clearly wrong... Hmm, actually wait: per the settled case: \"In 'I walked the team through Sales …; … so as a first pass make the matrix colour scale transparent green to blue, then we revisit next week, and give it the title …', 'and' cannot join 'give' with 'I walked' across 'make'.\" — this message n=132 is EXACTLY that pattern! \"I showed Budget split round the team; they didn't argue..., so as a first pass make the legend smaller, then we revisit next week; plus show the percentages\" — \"plus show the percentages\" — \"plus\" cannot join \"show\" with \"I showed\" across \"make\" (c4)! The settled case says this fails: L3. Here k2 \"plus\" joins c1 (I showed), c2 (they argued), c5 (we revisit), c6 (show) — with c4 (make the legend smaller) between c2/c5 and c6, belonging to c5 via \"so\"... The settled case: \"and give it the title\" — \"and\" joins \"give\" with... in that example the coordination was presumably joining give with walked across make — fails. Here \"plus\" joins show with showed across make — per the settled case → L3. Note: \"'plus' joins 'show the percentages' with 'I showed' across 'make the legend smaller'\". L3.\n\n**n=133**: \"sorry to nag, but how about z axis upright again? and let's have the y axis inverted on the surface chart\"\n- c1 fragment discourse \"sorry\". r1 \"to\" from c1 to c2 (nonfinite \"nag\"). k1 \"but\",\"and\" conjunct c1, c3, c4. c3 fragment modifier \"how\". r2 \"about\" from c3 to n1 (\"z axis upright again\" — dependents upright/again!). c4 imperative \"let\" complement n2 (\"'s\"). c5 nonfinite \"have\". n3 \"the y axis\". c6 nonfinite \"inverted\" subject n3. r3 \"on\" from c6 to n4 (\"the surface chart\"). r4 c4→c5. r5 c5→c6.\n- Issues: (a) n1 \"z axis upright again\" — \"upright\" and \"again\" as dependents of \"axis\" — settled: \"A word after a nominal that is a complement of its clause and says how the thing should be is a modifier of the clause... 'the dog a size bigger'... Held as a dependent of the nominal... L3\" — \"z axis upright again\" — \"how about z axis upright again?\" — \"upright\" says how the axis should be → modifier of the clause (c3, the fragment) — held as dependent of \"axis\" → L3. Also \"again\" as dependent — same. Note: \"'upright' and 'again' are held as dependents of 'z axis', but the words say the axis should be upright again\". L3.\n- (b) \"sorry to nag\" — c1 fragment discourse \"sorry\" with r1 \"to\" from c1 to \"nag\" — \"sorry to nag\" — plausible. k1 \"but\",\"and\" joins c1, c3, c4 — \"but how about...? and let's have...\" — the \"but\" and \"and\" — the message has \"but\" and \"and\" — fine. L3 from (a).\n\n**n=134**: \"let there be an ocena in the background\"\n- c1 imperative verb \"let\". n1 \"there\". c2 nonfinite \"be\" subject n1 complement n2 (\"an ocena\"). r1 \"in\" (ambiguous) from c2 to n3 (\"the background\"). r2 from c1 to c2.\n- \"let there be X\" — \"let\" verb imperative, \"there\" — per the let's settled case, \"'s' or 'us' is a nominal that is its complement\" — here \"there\" as subject of \"be\" — \"let there be an ocena\" — existential — \"there\" as subject of \"be\" is standard. Fine. L8.\n\n**n=135**: \"set o1 to 150 wide and 120 high\"\n- c1 imperative \"set\" complement n1 (\"o1\"). r1 \"to\" from c1 to n2 (\"150\" with dependents wide/and/120/high — head \"150\", dependents \"wide\",\"and\",\"120\",\"high\").\n- \"set o1 to 150 wide and 120 high\" — the circuit made \"150 wide and 120 high\" one nominal with head \"150\" and dependents wide/and/120/high. Issues: \"and\" as dependent — conjunction between \"wide\" and \"120 high\" — hmm, \"150 wide and 120 high\" — the \"and\" joins \"150 wide\" and \"120 high\" — two measures. Held as dependent of \"150\" — L3? \"A linking word is held as a modifier or a dependent\" — \"and\" is a conjunction — per \"flat and dry\" case, a conjunction between two modifier words of one clause is discourse. Here \"and\" between \"wide\" and \"120 high\" — held as dependent of the nominal \"150\" — wrong wire → L3. Also \"wide\"/\"high\" as dependents of \"150\" — \"150 wide\" — a measure with the graded word — per settled: \"A nominal whose head is the graded word ('a bit longer' with head 'longer')... fails: L3\" — here head is \"150\" (the measure), dependents include \"wide\" (the graded word) — hmm, \"150 wide\" — the head is the number, \"wide\" dependent — the failure case was head being the graded word; here head is the number — \"150 wide\" as a nominal (head 150, dependent wide) — like \"x 120\" — plausible measure nominal. But \"and 120 high\" merged into the same nominal — \"150 wide and 120 high\" as ONE nominal with head \"150\" — merges two measures (\"150 wide\" and \"120 high\") → \"two nominals are merged\" → L3. Note: \"'150 wide and 120 high' is merged into one nominal with head '150', but the words give two measures, '150 wide' and '120 high', joined by 'and'\". L3.\n\n**n=136**: \"let there be a medium cloud on the very top edge, and a big bench down at the bottom — exactly where the picture needs it\"\n- c1 imperative \"let\". n1 \"there\". c2 nonfinite \"be\" modifiers \"down\",\"exactly\" subject n1 complement k1. n2 \"a medium cloud\". r1 \"on\" from n2 to n3 (\"the very top edge\"). k1 \"and\" conjunct n2, n4. n4 \"a big bench\". r2 \"at\" from c2 to n5 (\"the bottom\"). r3 \"where\" from c2 to c3 (\"needs\" subject \"the picture\" complement \"it\"). r4 from c1 to c2.\n- \"let there be a medium cloud on the very top edge, and a big bench down at the bottom\" — c2 \"be\" with complement k1 = {cloud, bench} — both under \"be\" — fine (\"let there be X and Y\"). \"down\" as modifier of c2 — \"a big bench down at the bottom\" — \"down\" describes the bench's position — held as modifier of the \"be\" clause — hmm, \"down\" could be a dependent of \"bench\" — settled: \"A word of place after a nominal may be a dependent of that nominal or a modifier of the clause: grammar lets it describe either\" — \"down\" after \"a big bench\" — wait, \"down\" is before \"at the bottom\", right after \"bench\" — \"a big bench down at the bottom\" — \"down\" as modifier of the clause — allowed (\"near the lake there\" case). Fine. \"exactly\" modifier — fine. r3 \"where\" from c2 — \"exactly where the picture needs it\" — fine. L8.\n\n**n=137**: \"shift the same woman in the red dress all the way over to the very left\"\n- c1 imperative \"shift\" modifier \"over\" complements n1 (\"the same woman\"), n3 (\"all the way\"). r1 \"in\" from n1 to n2 (\"the red dress\"). r2 \"to\" from c1 to n4 (\"the very left\").\n- \"all the way\" as a nominal complement of \"shift\"?? \"shift the same woman ... all the way over to the very left\" — \"all the way\" is a measure/modifier of the shift — held as complement of \"shift\" — states the verb acts on \"all the way\" — wrong? Per the time-phrase case analog — \"a coordination with what the verb acts on states a second thing acted on: L3\" — here a second complement. Hmm — \"shift the woman all the way\" — \"all the way\" is not acted on. L3? Or acceptable as a measure nominal tied to the verb? \"move block 6 two spaces left\" — \"two spaces\" is