\"a verb after 'and' or 'then' that follows a clause with a subject and an auxiliary, in the form that auxiliary takes, may share them... Written as a command, imperative, it passes too.\" — the preceding clause c5 \"I'll take it from there\" has subject \"I\" and auxiliary \"'ll\" — \"put\" after \"and then\" following a clause with subject and auxiliary — \"in the form that auxiliary takes\" — \"'ll\" takes bare infinitive \"take\" — so \"put\" would be nonfinite sharing \"I'll\" — \"I'll take it from there and then put the y axis on the right\" — YES — \"and then put\" shares \"I'll\" — so c6 could be nonfinite/declarative with no subject — the circuit wrote declarative with no subject — that matches \"a clause with no subject of its own, declarative or nonfinite\" — PASSES per the settled case! Good — c6 declarative (no subject) is allowed.\n- (c) k1 \"and\",\"so\",\"and\",\"and\" joins c1, c2, c4, c5, c6 — the message: \"...pulled..., and left..., so make the colour map blue to lighter blue and I'll take it from there and then put the y axis on the right\" — the conjunctions: \"and\" (pulled and left), \"so\" (so make), \"and\" (blue and I'll take), \"and\" (there and then put). k1 uses and/so/and/and with conjuncts c1, c2, c4, c5, c6 — five conjuncts, four conjunctions — matches the message's four conjunctions joining five clauses! c1-pulled, c2-left, c4-make, c5-take, c6-put — yes: \"pulled and left, so make ... and I'll take ... and then put\" — the joins: and(pulled,left), so(left→make? or make is result), and(make,take), and(take,put). The coordination k1 with all five — plausible. L8.\n\n**n=139**: \"guide block 10 left of block 9, and after that, guide block 10 further left until it is even with block 9\"\n- c1 imperative \"guide\" modifiers \"left\",\"further\",\"left\" complement k1. n1 \"block\", n2 \"10\". r1 \"of\" (ambiguous) from c1 to n3 (\"block\"). n4 \"9\". k1 \"and\" conjunct n1, n6. r2 \"after\" from n6 to n5 (\"that\"). n6 \"guide block\" (dependent guide, head block!). n7 \"10\". r3 \"until\" from c1 to c2 (\"is\" modifier \"even\" subject \"it\"). r4 \"with\" from c2 to n9 (\"block\"). n10 \"9\". r5 n1→n2, r6 n3→n4, r7 n6→n7, r8 n9→n10.\n- Issues: (a) n6 \"guide block\" — \"guide\" held as dependent of \"block\" — the second \"guide block 10\" — \"guide\" is a verb (command) — held as dependent of a nominal → the command lost? The message: \"guide block 10 left of block 9, and after that, guide block 10 further left until...\" — two \"guide\" commands. The circuit made ONE clause c1 (guide) with modifiers left/further/left and complement k1 = {block 10 (n1), \"guide block\" 10 (n6)} — so the second \"guide\" is a dependent of \"block\" — the second command's verb is not a verb of any clause → L5? \"its verb is held as ... a dependent ... and no clause has it as verb\" — \"guide\" (second) held as dependent of n6, no clause has it as verb → L5. Hmm, but there IS a clause with \"guide\" as verb (c1) — the first one. L5 says \"no clause has it as verb\" — the second \"guide\" word — no clause has THE SECOND \"guide\" as verb. Each word stands in one value; the second \"guide\" is a dependent. The message's second command \"guide block 10 further left until...\" is represented as a nominal \"guide block 10\" coordinated with the first \"block 10\" under one \"guide\" clause — reading: \"guide block 10 ... and guide block 10 ...\" — the coordination k1 joins the two block-nominals as joint objects of one \"guide\" — that's actually a plausible reading! \"guide block 10 left of block 9 and [guide] block 10 further left...\" — with \"after that\" attached to n6. Hmm — but then the modifiers \"further left\" are on c1 with \"left\" — lumped. Eh. Plausible continuation? The next layer sees one guide command with two objects and modifiers left/further/left — the sequence lost-ish but... \n- (b) r1 \"of\" from c1 to \"block 9\" — \"left of block 9\" — per settled: \"'left of the hill': 'left of' may be one marker, or 'left' a nominal or clause modifier with the relation marked 'of' starting at the clause or at 'left'.\" Here \"left\" is a modifier of c1 and r1 \"of\" from c1 (the clause) to block 9 — matches the allowed option. Good.\n- (c) \"until it is even with block 9\" — r3 \"until\" from c1 to c2 — fine.\n- So the questionable part is the second \"guide\" as dependent. Per the settled command case, \"guide\" as first word can be noun or verb → verb; held as noun in a fragment → L1. But here the second \"guide\" is not the first word of the message... it's the first word of the second command after \"and after that,\". Hmm. The reading \"guide [block 10 left of block 9] and [after that, guide block 10 further left...]\" — as joint objects of one guide — \"guide A and B\" — plausible! Like \"point the big bear toward the pond, and a huge bear...\" (n=26) where the second was a fragment with ellipsis. Here instead of ellipsis, the circuit coordinated the objects under one verb. That's a legitimate alternative reading? \"guide block 10 left of block 9, and after that, guide block 10 further left\" — coordinating \"block 10\" (first) and \"guide block 10\" (second, with 'guide' inside) — the \"guide\" in the second is then redundant... I think it's a wrong statement: the second \"guide\" is a command verb held as a dependent. But is it \"lost explicit information\"? The command force of the second \"guide\" — the circuit has \"after that\" tying n6 to \"that\"... The next layer sees: guide (block 10, guide block 10) with left/further/left until even — it can reconstruct. Hmm.\n- I'll grade L5? or L8? The rule for rejection: wrong statement or lost explicit info. Holding the second \"guide\" as a dependent of \"block\" — the words \"guide block 10\" as a nominal means \"the block 10 that guides\"?? — a wrong statement (states block 10 is a guide). Hmm, like n=79 \"halve teh Shelve label size\" → L1. And n=45 \"label number n4\" → L1. Those were first-word commands. Here mid-message. I'll go L8 actually — because the coordination reading \"guide A and B\" is plausible and the next layer can continue... no wait. Ugh.\n- Let me think about what's cleanest: the message has two commands. The circuit wrote one clause (c1) and coordinated the two objects. The second \"guide\" word is inside a nominal. Per L5: \"A clause of the message is not written: its verb is held as a modifier, a dependent, a head or a discourse word, and no clause has it as verb.\" The second command's verb \"guide\" is held as a dependent (of \"block\"), and no clause has that word as verb → L5. I'll go L5. Note: \"the second 'guide' in 'guide block 10 further left' is held as a dependent of 'block', so that command has no verb\". L5.\n\n**n=140**: \"draw a house, at x 120, at y 90, 160 wide, 100 high, size 80, coloured red, labelled Home\"\n- c1 imperative \"draw\" modifier \"wide\" complements n1 (\"a house\"), n6 (\"160\"). n1 \"a house\". r1 \"at\" from n1 to n2 (\"x\"). n3 \"120\". r2 \"at\" from n1 to n4 (\"y\"). n5 \"90\". n6 \"160\". n7 \"100 high\" (dependent high, head 100). n8 \"size\". n9 \"80\". c2 nonfinite \"coloured\" modifier \"red\". c3 nonfinite \"labelled\" complement n10 (\"Home\"). r3 n1→n7, r4 n1→n8, r5 n2→n3, r6 n4→n5, r7 n8→n9, r8 n8→c2, r9 n8→c3.\n- Issues: (a) \"160 wide\" — n6 \"160\" as complement of c1 with modifier \"wide\" on c1 — \"160 wide\" — the measure 160 with \"wide\" — \"wide\" as modifier of the draw clause — eh, plausible (\"draw a house 160 wide\"). (b) \"100 high\" — n7 head \"100\" dependent \"high\" — fine. (c) \"coloured red\" — c2 nonfinite \"coloured\" tied from n8 (\"size\")!! r8 from n8 to c2 — \"size 80 coloured red\" — the coloured attaches to \"size\" — should attach to the house (n1). Wrong end? \"A relation has the wrong end\" — L3. Hmm — \"draw a house, ..., size 80, coloured red, labelled Home\" — \"coloured red\" describes the house. The circuit ties c2 and c3 to n8 (\"size\") — stating the SIZE is coloured 