rse. In a nominal, \"fourth and fifth\" — dependents aren't wires either — so \"and\" can't coordinate them — where does it go? Discourse? Or as a dependent? Hmm — if the schema has no wire for the pair, the options are discourse or... holding it as a dependent of the head. The \"flat and dry\" case chose discourse for clause modifiers. For nominal dependents, holding \"and\" as a dependent is arguably \"where it stands\". Ugh. Let me compare n=22: \"three and a half widths\" — n3 has dependent \"and\" — \"three and a half widths\" — \"and\" as dependent of \"widths\" — I accepted that (n=22 is L5 anyway). And n=35: \"blue, blue, green, red\" — no \"and\". n=90: \"orange jacket and blue hat\" — k1 coordination with \"and\" — proper. n=85: \"fourth and fifth figures\" — \"and\" as dependent. Given n=22 uses \"and\" as dependent in \"three and a half\" (a number phrase, where \"and\" is part of the number), \"fourth and fifth\" is different — two separate adjectives. Hmm. I'll soften: accept L8 for n=85? The instruction says judge fairly, reject only wrong statement or lost info. Holding \"and\" as a dependent of \"figures\" — no info lost, no wrong statement really (the nominal \"the fourth and fifth figures\" reads naturally). I'll switch n=85 to L8. Hmm... but then n=135's \"and\" as dependent (\"150 wide and 120 high\") — there the issue is merging two measures into one nominal, which is a real merge (two nominals merged → L3). For n=85, \"the fourth and fifth figures\" as one nominal — \"fourth and fifth\" both modify \"figures\" — one nominal is fine. Yes, L8 for n=85.\n\nSimilarly reconsider n=80: \"15, 15 for 1 star and Sample\" — n4 = \"15 15 for 1 star\" head \"star\" — this merges \"15\", \"15\", \"for 1 star\"... \"values 15, 15 for 1 star and Sample\" — the values list: \"15\", \"15 for 1 star\", \"Sample\"? Garbled. The circuit: n4 head \"star\" with dependents \"15\",\"15\",\"for\",\"1\" — \"for\" as dependent — \"for\" is a preposition (linking word) →