he entire scene' and 'mind' as conjuncts, but the words only add them after 'dress the scene up'\". Hmm — I need the note to quote message words. \"'the entire scene' and 'mind' are joined by 'then', but the words put them after 'dress the scene up as first light all the way through'\". OK.\n\nReconsider n=54 (L3): merged nominal n6 (nothing else of the house, everything else untouched). Keep L3.\n\nReconsider n=57 (L3): \"of\" as modifier. Keep L3.\n\nReconsider n=37 (L3): \"tree\" as modifier of c5, \"Proposal\" as complement — splits the name \"Proposal tree\". Keep L3.\n\nReconsider n=132 (L3): \"plus\" coordination across \"make\". Per the settled case example (which is nearly verbatim this message pattern), L3. Keep.\n\nReconsider n=133 (L3): \"upright again\" as dependents of \"z axis\". Keep L3.\n\nReconsider n=140 (L3): coloured/labelled tied to \"size\" instead of the house. Keep L3.\n\nReconsider n=139 (L5): second \"guide\" as dependent. Keep L5.\n\nReconsider n=141 (L1): \"centre\" as head of nominal. Keep L1.\n\nReconsider n=130 (L1): \"top\" as imperative verb. Hmm — \"top five regions only\" — \"top\" as verb of imperative \"top five regions!\" — grammar allows \"top\" as a verb... but the part \"top five regions only\" after a comma — is it a command? The message: \"Revenue by month of the order date, top five regions only\" — clearly a noun phrase (restriction). \"top\" is an adjective here. Made the verb of an imperative clause → L1 per \"a noun made its verb is L1\" analog / \"verb is a word that is no verb here\". Keep L1.\n\nReconsider n=116 (L1): \"rides/repairs/spares\" as verbs. Keep L1. Note: the message words: 'rides', 'repairs', 'spares'.\n\nReconsider n=126 (L1): c2 \"argued\" interrogative; c6 \"borders\" as verb. Keep L1.\n\nReconsider n=107 (L1): c8 \"make the bars blue\" declarative with subject \"the top six regions\". Keep L1.\n\nReconsider n=94 (L1): c2 \"leave the path clear\" declarative. Keep L1.\n\nReconsider n=67/n=112 (L1): \"I need...\" / \"I'd like...\" written interrogative. Keep L1.\n\nReconsider n=114 (L1): \"not\" as modifier. Keep L1.\n\nReconsider n=72 (L1): \"smaler\" as verb. Keep L1.\n\nReconsider n=123 (L1): \"left\" as verb. Keep L1. Hmm — one more thought on n=123: \"then slide block 15 left to block 14\" — c3 fragment (complement \"slide block 15\", ellipsis c1) — so the \"slide\" of the second command is a dependent of \"block\" (n5), and \"left\" is the verb of c2 nonfinite tied from n5. So the second command \"slide block 15 left to block 14\" is represented as fragment \"slide block 15\" + nonfinite \"left\" clause. The verb \"slide\" (2nd) is a dependent — L5-ish; and \"left\" as verb — L1-ish. First applicable: L1 (verb is a word that is no verb here — \"left\" is not a verb in this context... though \"left\" is a verb form of \"leave\"). Hmm — \"slide block 15 left to block 14\" — could \"left\" be the verb \"leave\"? \"block 15 left to block 14\" — no, \"slide ... left\" — left is a particle/direction. The settled case \"move it left\" — \"left\" is a modifier. So \"left\" as verb here is wrong → L1. Keep L1. Note: \"'left' in 'slide block 15 left to block 14' is a direction, but it is written as the verb of a nonfinite clause\".\n\nReconsider n=43 (L1): \"blocks\" as declarative verb. Keep L1.\n\nReconsider n=45 (L1): \"label\" as dependent. Keep L1.\n\nReconsider n=79 (L1): \"halve\" as dependent. Keep L1.\n\nReconsider n=87 (L1): \"change\" as dependent. Keep L1.\n\nReconsider n=91 (L1): \"rotate\" as head of nominal, \"dwn\" as verb. Keep L1.\n\nReconsider n=18 (L1): \"sidee\" as head of nominal. Keep L1.\n\nReconsider n=41 (L1): \"Let\" as interrogative verb, \"us\" as complement of \"show\". Keep L1.\n\nReconsider n=93 (L1): \"hollow\" as declarative verb. Keep L1.\n\nNow the L8 list: 1,2,3,5,6,7,8,9,11,14,15,17,20,21,24,25,26,27,28,30,31,32,33,34,35,36,38,39,40,42,46,49,50,51,52,53,55,56,58,59,60,61,63,64,65,66?no—66 is L3,68,69,70,71,74,75,77,78,81,82,83,84,85,86,88,89,90?no—90 is L3,92,95,96,97,98,99?no—99 is L3,100,101,102,103,104,105,106,108,109,110,111,113,115,117,118,119,120,121,122,124,125,127,128,129,131,134,136,137,138,142,143,144?no—L3,146,147,148,149,150.\n\nLet me also double check a few I marked L8 but have doubts:\n\nn=11: c1 nonfinite \"facing\" subject n1 — untied by relation. Settled: \"A participle or a subordinate clause tied to nothing is L6. Any relation to its clause or to the nominal it describes is enough.\" c1 has subject=$n1 — is that \"a relation to the nominal it describes\"? The subject wire connects c1 to n1 within the circuit. Hmm — strictly, \"any relation\" means syntax.relation. c1 has no relation pointing to it or from it. Its only link is the subject slot. Is the subject slot a \"tie\"? L6: \"A clause or a nominal is tied to nothing inside its sentence.\" — c1's subject is n1, so c1 is tied to n1 inside its sentence via the subject. I'd say tied. Also k1? No — n=11 has no k1... let me check: n=11 wires: n1, r1, r2, c1, n4, c2, c3, r3. c1: subject=$n1 — no relation touches c1. Hmm. Risky. But \"all facing right\" — the clause's subject is the kites — the circuit graph connects them. I'll accept L8.\n\nn=25: c1 nonfinite \"growing\" subject n2 — same situation — plus k1 coordination with c2. Tied via subject and coordination. L8.\n\nn=117: c1 \"Could\" interrogative verb with subject \"you\" — k1 joins c1, c2. Tied. L8.\n\nn=46: r1 \"until\" from c3 to c1 — c1 is \"the row is back...\" — tied. c3 fragment complement n1 (\"quick question\"), r4 n1→n2. c3 tied via r1. OK L8.\n\nn=120: \"add a cat 260 Take 2\" — c2 imperative \"Take\" complements n2 (\"260\"), n3 (\"2\"); k1 joins c1, c2. \"260\" is complement of \"Take\" — but \"a cat 260\" pairing — \"260\" not tied to \"cat\". L6? \"A pairing the words give is not written: 'x 120' with '120' in another wire and tied to nothing\" — here \"260\" is tied (to Take's clause as complement) — not \"tied to nothing\". The pairing cat-260 not